5","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"first-class"},"991":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-36","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"shares numpy's global RNG"},"992":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-37","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"short scripts, REPL exploration"},"993":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-38","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#three-ways-to-sample","content":"All three charge FLOPs to the active `BudgetContext`. The difference is *where the random state lives*."},"994":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-39","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#default_rng--canonical-pattern","content":"The returned object is a `numpy.random.Generator` subclass — `isinstance(rng, np.random.Generator)` holds, so it works anywhere a numpy `Generator` is expected."},"995":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-40","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#randomstate--legacy-compat","content":"The legacy `numpy.random.RandomState` API is still supported with full FLOP counting:"},"996":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-41","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#randomstate--legacy-compat","content":"Use this only when you're porting code that already uses `RandomState`. New code should prefer `default_rng()`."},"997":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-42","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#module-level-samplers","content":"`fnp.random.randn`, `normal`, `uniform`, `choice`, `shuffle`, etc. are kept first-class for short scripts and REPL use:"},"998":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-43","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#module-level-samplers","content":"These match `numpy.random` semantics exactly, plus FLOP accounting. **They share numpy's global RNG state**, which is fine for one-shot scripts but creates a foot-gun in longer-running programs — see Pitfall: global state below."},"999":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-44","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#cross-api-parity","content":"Same physical operation charges identical FLOPs regardless of which API you used:"},"1000":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-45","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#cross-api-parity","content":"`default_rng().normal(...)` inherits the calibrated weight of the module-level `random.normal` it shadows. No per-method calibration is needed for new method-level entries — runtime aliasing handles it."},"1001":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-46","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#pickle-and-copy","content":"Counted RNGs round-trip cleanly through `pickle` and `copy`:"},"1002":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-47","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#spawn-for-parallel-work","content":"`Generator.spawn(n)` returns `n` independent counted children that share no state with the parent:"},"1003":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-48","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#spawn-for-parallel-work","content":"Each child has its own `bit_generator`, so children running in parallel produce independent streams — no contention, no shared state."},"1004":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-49","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#constructing-from-a-specific-bitgenerator","content":"The `Generator(BitGenerator(seed))` idiom from numpy works identically:"},"1005":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-50","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#constructing-from-a-specific-bitgenerator","content":"The bit-generator classes (`BitGenerator`, `MT19937`, `PCG64`, `PCG64DXSM`, `Philox`, `SFC64`) pass through to numpy unchanged — they don't do math, so there's nothing to count at the bit-generator level. FLOPs are charged at the sampler-method level on the resulting `Generator`."},"1006":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-51","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#pitfall-global-state","content":"The one path that *doesn't* give per-instance isolation is the module-level samplers, because they share numpy's global RNG. Across two sequential `BudgetContext`s:"},"1007":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-52","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#pitfall-global-state","content":"If you need each context to be independently reproducible, either re-seed at the top of each context, or use `default_rng()`:"},"1008":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-53","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#pitfall-global-state","content":"Note that **`BudgetContext`s cannot be nested** at all (the runtime raises `RuntimeError(\"Cannot nest BudgetContexts\")`), so the global-state issue only affects *sequential* contexts, never overlapping ones."},"1009":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-54","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#forward-compatibility-integration-with-other-apis","content":"Anything that accepts a numpy `Generator` accepts a counted `Generator`. For example, when `flopscope.stats` grows an `rvs()` method (random variate sampling), it will accept a counted RNG and inherit FLOP counting for free:"},"1010":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-55","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#forward-compatibility-integration-with-other-apis","content":"The pattern works because `rng.uniform(...)` and `fnp.stats.norm.ppf(...)` are both already FLOP-counted. Inverse-CDF sampling threads naturally through the existing wrappers."},"1011":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-56","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"Pattern"},"1012":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-57","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"Status"},"1013":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-58","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"Why"},"1014":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-59","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"Nested `BudgetContext`s"},"1015":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-60","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"runtime error"},"1016":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-61","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"Out of scope; flopscope models a single budget per program region."},"1017":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-62","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"Methods not in the registry"},"1018":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-63","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"`UnsupportedFunctionError`"},"1019":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-64","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"Closes the silent-bypass hole from issue #18. New numpy methods are added to the registry on each version bump (CI: `scripts/numpy_audit.py --ci`)."},"1020":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-65","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"`numpy.random.<X>` directly"},"1021":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-66","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"not counted"},"1022":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-67","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#whats-deliberately-not-supported","content":"Use `fnp.random.<X>` for counting. `numpy.random.X` continues to work but bypasses flopscope."},"1023":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-68","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#related","content":"`fnp.random.default_rng` — canonical entry point (op page)"},"1024":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-69","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#related","content":"Migrate from NumPy — broader migration guide"},"1025":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-70","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#related","content":"Issue #18 — design rationale and history"},"1026":{"id":"/docs/guides/random-71","page_id":"/docs/guides/random","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/random#related","content":"(`fnp.random.RandomState` is a class constructor, not a registered op — its\nsampler methods have individual op pages under `/docs/api/numpy/random/`.)"},"1027":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","type":"page","content":"Saving & Loading Models","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/save-load"},"1028":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/save-load#the-mental-model","content":"The mental model"},"1029":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/save-load#saving-and-loading-arrays","content":"Saving and loading arrays"},"1030":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/save-load#custom-models-with-flopsmodule","content":"Custom models with flops.Module"},"1031":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"How `state_dict` auto-discovery works"},"1032":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/save-load#config-and-reconstruction","content":"`config()` and reconstruction"},"1033":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/save-load#in-place-loading-with-load","content":"In-place loading with `load`"},"1034":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/save-load#authoring-weight-files-offline","content":"Authoring weight files offline"},"1035":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/save-load#faq-why-cant-load-return-my-model-object-directly","content":"FAQ: why can't `load` return my model object directly?"},"1036":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-8","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load","content":"*Store weights in a portable, pickle-free file. Loading them back is free; saving bills the bytes you write.*"},"1037":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-9","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load","content":"**You will learn:**"},"1038":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-10","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load","content":"What a flopscope file contains and why it is safe to load"},"1039":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-11","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load","content":"How to save and load individual arrays with `fnp.save`/`fnp.savez`/`fnp.load`"},"1040":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-12","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load","content":"How to build a custom model with `flops.Module` and round-trip it through a file"},"1041":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-13","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load","content":"How to prepare weight files offline with plain NumPy and load them during the challenge run"},"1042":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-14","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#the-mental-model","content":"A flopscope file stores exactly two things:"},"1043":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-15","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#the-mental-model","content":"**Named numeric arrays** — floating-point, integer, bool, or complex; no object dtype."},"1044":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-16","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#the-mental-model","content":"**An inert JSON `__meta__` block** — plain key/value data, never executed."},"1045":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-17","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#the-mental-model","content":"Your *class* always lives in *your code*, never in the file. flopscope reads the numeric payload and hands the values to the object you construct — no pickle, no `eval`, no `exec`. That boundary is the entire security model: loading a file can never execute code."},"1046":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-18","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#the-mental-model","content":"Loading is **free**: `fnp.load` charges 0 FLOPs regardless of file size. Saving is\nnot — `fnp.save`, `fnp.savez`, and `fnp.savez_compressed` each charge 4 FLOPs per\nelement written, plus 4 FLOPs per byte of the array names themselves and of a\nserialized `__meta__` block, so there is no free way to smuggle data through any of\nthose channels."},"1047":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-19","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#saving-and-loading-arrays","content":"Use `fnp.savez` to write multiple named arrays into a single `.npz` file:"},"1048":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-20","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#saving-and-loading-arrays","content":"Load them back with `fnp.load`, which returns a plain `dict`:"},"1049":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-21","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#saving-and-loading-arrays","content":"For a single array, use the `.npy` variants:"},"1050":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-22","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#saving-and-loading-arrays","content":"Use `fnp.savez_compressed` when file size matters — it writes the same format as\n`fnp.savez` but with lossless compression:"},"1051":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-23","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#saving-and-loading-arrays","content":"> **Note:** flopscope refuses `object` dtype everywhere, not just at save —\n> building one with `fnp.array(..., dtype=object)` already raises\n> `UnsupportedDtypeError`. See the object-dtype note in the FLOP counting\n> model. The file format itself\n> accepts only numeric dtypes (float, int, bool, complex); `str_`, `bytes_`,\n> `datetime64`, `timedelta64`, and structured dtypes are rejected by `save`\n> with a `ValueError` even though they are billable everywhere else."},"1052":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-24","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#custom-models-with-flopsmodule","content":"For whole models, `flops.Module` auto-discovers every array attribute and\nnested sub-module, so you never write a manual save loop."},"1053":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-25","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"`flops.Module.state_dict()` walks `vars(self)` and collects:"},"1054":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-26","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"Attribute type"},"1055":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-27","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"How it is collected"},"1056":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-28","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"`FlopscopeArray`"},"1057":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-29","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"stored directly under the attribute name"},"1058":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-30","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"Nested `flops.Module`"},"1059":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-31","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"recursed with a dotted prefix (`layers.0.W`)"},"1060":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-32","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"`list` / `tuple` of the above"},"1061":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-33","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"indexed as `layers.0`, `layers.1`, …"},"1062":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-34","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"`dict` of the above"},"1063":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-35","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"keyed as `config.key`"},"1064":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-36","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"Attributes starting with `_`"},"1065":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-37","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"**skipped**"},"1066":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-38","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"Everything else (scalars, strings, …)"},"1067":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-39","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#how-state_dict-auto-discovery-works","content":"**skipped**"},"1068":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-40","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#config-and-reconstruction","content":"Override `config()` to return the keyword arguments that `__init__` needs to\nreconstruct your model with the right shapes. `from_file` passes them verbatim:"},"1069":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-41","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#in-place-loading-with-load","content":"If you already have a model with the right architecture, call `load` to update\nits weights in place:"},"1070":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-42","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#authoring-weight-files-offline","content":"You can prepare weight files in your regular development environment (real NumPy,\nGPU frameworks, etc.) and load them inside the budgeted competition run:"},"1071":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-43","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#authoring-weight-files-offline","content":"`fnp.load` reads files written by plain `numpy.save`/`numpy.savez` without any\nformat conversion. The only constraint is that the arrays must be numeric dtype."},"1072":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-44","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#faq-why-cant-load-return-my-model-object-directly","content":"Returning a live model object from a file would require encoding the *class* into\nthe file. The only general way to do that is pickle — and pickle can execute\narbitrary code at load time. flopscope deliberately avoids this."},"1073":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-45","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#faq-why-cant-load-return-my-model-object-directly","content":"Instead, the class always comes from *your code*:"},"1074":{"id":"/docs/guides/save-load-46","page_id":"/docs/guides/save-load","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/save-load#faq-why-cant-load-return-my-model-object-directly","content":"`from_file` is a class method: you call it on `MLP`, so Python already knows\nthe class. The file only supplies the weights (numeric arrays) and the\nJSON config. No code ever travels through the file."},"1075":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"page","content":"Symmetry Savings","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry"},"1076":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"Why symmetry matters"},"1077":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"1078":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#how-to-declare-symmetry","content":"How to declare symmetry"},"1079":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#full-symmetry-with-symmetrygroupsymmetric","content":"Full symmetry with `SymmetryGroup.symmetric`"},"1080":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#multiple-independent-full-symmetric-groups","content":"Multiple independent full symmetric groups"},"1081":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#explicit-full-symmetric-groups-with-symmetrygroupsymmetric","content":"Explicit full symmetric groups with `SymmetryGroup.symmetric`"},"1082":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#cyclic-symmetry-with-symmetrygroupcyclic","content":"Cyclic symmetry with `SymmetryGroup.cyclic`"},"1083":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#dihedral-symmetry-with-symmetrygroupdihedral","content":"Dihedral symmetry with `SymmetryGroup.dihedral`"},"1084":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-8","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#arbitrary-subgroups-from-custom-generators","content":"Arbitrary subgroups from custom generators"},"1085":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-9","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#multiple-explicit-groups-on-one-tensor","content":"Multiple explicit groups on one tensor"},"1086":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-10","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"Generating example data with the Reynolds operator"},"1087":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-11","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"Symmetry propagation at a glance"},"1088":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-12","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"Slicing rules"},"1089":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-13","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#reduction-rules","content":"Reduction rules"},"1090":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-14","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#binary-pointwise-ops-and-broadcasting","content":"Binary pointwise ops and broadcasting"},"1091":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-15","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#warnings-conservative-behavior-and-re-tagging","content":"Warnings, conservative behavior, and re-tagging"},"1092":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-16","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#edge-cases-when-symmetrictensors-meet-numpy-protocols","content":"Edge cases when SymmetricTensors meet NumPy protocols"},"1093":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-17","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#in-place-dunders-refuse-symmetry-corrupting-writes","content":"In-place dunders refuse symmetry-corrupting writes"},"1094":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-18","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#in-place-sort--partition-refuse-on-symmetrictensor","content":"In-place sort / partition refuse on SymmetricTensor"},"1095":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-19","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#ufuncat-refuses-on-symmetrictensor","content":"`ufunc.at` refuses on SymmetricTensor"},"1096":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-20","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#ufuncouter-produces-direct-product-symmetry","content":"`ufunc.outer` produces direct-product symmetry"},"1097":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-21","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#tensordot-keeps-surviving-direct-product-symmetry","content":"`tensordot` keeps surviving direct-product symmetry"},"1098":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-22","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#multi-output-ufuncs-preserve-symmetry-on-every-output","content":"Multi-output ufuncs preserve symmetry on every output"},"1099":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-23","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#cost-model-is-a-placeholder-above-the-dimino_budget-group-order","content":"Cost model is a placeholder above the `dimino_budget` group order"},"1100":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-24","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"Under the hood"},"1101":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-25","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#going-deeper","content":"Going deeper"},"1102":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-26","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry","content":"*Reduce FLOP costs when your tensors have symmetry.*"},"1103":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-27","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry","content":"**You will learn:**"},"1104":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-28","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry","content":"How to declare full and non-full tensor symmetries with `flops.as_symmetric()`"},"1105":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-29","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry","content":"How to generate example tensors for arbitrary permutation groups"},"1106":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-30","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry","content":"When to use `fnp.random.symmetric()` (sample + project) versus `flops.symmetrize()`\n(project existing data)"},"1107":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-31","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry","content":"How slicing, reductions, and binary pointwise ops preserve, weaken, or drop symmetry metadata"},"1108":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-32","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry","content":"When to re-tag results with `flops.as_symmetric()` after conservative propagation"},"1109":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-33","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry","content":"> For the underlying mental model — orbits, unique work items, why these savings exist — see Symmetry-aware FLOP counting."},"1110":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-34","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"Many tensors contain repeated structure. A symmetric matrix has only\n`n * (n + 1) / 2` unique elements instead of `n^2`, and higher-order tensors\nwith permutation symmetry can shrink the effective element count even more.\nWhen Flopscope knows a tensor's symmetry, it charges FLOPs based on unique elements\ninstead of dense ones."},"1111":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-35","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"Operation"},"1112":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-36","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"Dense cost"},"1113":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-37","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"Symmetry-aware cost"},"1114":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-38","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"Why it drops"},"1115":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-39","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"`fnp.exp(s2_matrix)`"},"1116":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-40","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"`n^2`"},"1117":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-41","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"`n * (n + 1) / 2`"},"1118":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-42","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"only unique matrix entries matter"},"1119":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-43","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"`fnp.einsum('ki,kj->ij', x, x)` with `x` repeated, `m=n=4`"},"1120":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-44","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"128"},"1121":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-45","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"80"},"1122":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-46","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"the repeated `x` operand lets flopscope detect symmetric output and discount accumulations + multiplications"},"1123":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-47","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"`fnp.einsum('i,j,k->ijk', v, v, v)` with `v` repeated, `n=4`"},"1124":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-48","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"192"},"1125":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-49","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"60"},"1126":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-50","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"three identical operands induce an `S₃` action on the output labels"},"1127":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-51","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"`fnp.sum(s3_tensor)` on a fully-symmetric `(4, 4, 4)`"},"1128":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-52","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"63"},"1129":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-53","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"19"},"1130":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-54","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"the same orbit logic applies to reductions, not just einsum"},"1131":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-55","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#why-symmetry-matters","content":"By contrast, declaring `symmetry=` on an `einsum` output tags the result for downstream\noperations; it does not reduce that `einsum`'s own cost by itself."},"1132":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-56","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#quick-start","content":"`flops.as_symmetric()` validates the data first. Validation uses a tolerance,\nwhile the tag it grants is read as exact — the cost model prices every position\nin an orbit after the first as redundant and does not read the buffer again — so\n`as_symmetric` makes the two agree before tagging: each orbit takes the value at\nits lexicographically smallest index. Data that is already exactly invariant is\nreturned unchanged, so a genuinely symmetric array is tagged without being\ncopied; data that passed only within the tolerance is copied first, and the\nvalues in the discarded positions do not survive. Your own array is never\nmodified either way."},"1133":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-57","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#quick-start","content":"After that, Flopscope propagates symmetry metadata algebraically through many\noperations. Unary pointwise ops preserve symmetry-aware costs and keep the same exact group, including non-full groups such as `C_k` or `D_k`. Slicing, reductions, and binary pointwise ops can weaken it or remove it entirely."},"1134":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-58","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#full-symmetry-with-symmetrygroupsymmetric","content":"Use `SymmetryGroup.symmetric` when the tensor is invariant under every permutation of a\nset of axes."},"1135":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-59","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#full-symmetry-with-symmetrygroupsymmetric","content":"This is the most common declaration: full `S_2` symmetry on matrix axes\n`(0, 1)`."},"1136":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-60","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#multiple-independent-full-symmetric-groups","content":"This declares one full symmetric group on axes `(0, 1)` and another on\n`(2, 3)`."},"1137":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-61","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#explicit-full-symmetric-groups-with-symmetrygroupsymmetric","content":"This explicit group is useful once you want to inspect or combine symmetry directly."},"1138":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-62","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#cyclic-symmetry-with-symmetrygroupcyclic","content":"`C_3` means rotations are allowed, but reflections are not. This is weaker\nthan `S_3`, so it usually gives fewer savings."},"1139":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-63","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#dihedral-symmetry-with-symmetrygroupdihedral","content":"`D_4` includes both rotations and reflections of a four-position structure."},"1140":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-64","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#arbitrary-subgroups-from-custom-generators","content":"Use this form when the built-in constructors do not describe your symmetry."},"1141":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-65","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#multiple-explicit-groups-on-one-tensor","content":"This uses a direct product of two exact symmetry factors, one on `(0, 1)` and one on `(2, 3)`."},"1142":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-66","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"When you want example tensors for arbitrary groups, prefer `fnp.random.symmetric`.\nIt is a handy helper that samples from a distribution and applies the Reynolds\noperator.\nUse `flops.symmetrize` when you already have concrete data to symmetrize, and\n`flops.as_symmetric` when you already have concrete data to validate and tag."},"1143":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-67","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"`R_G(T) = (1 / |G|) * sum_{g in G} g · T`"},"1144":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-68","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"This helper is ideal for docs, tests, and experiments:"},"1145":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-69","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"it works for `S_k`, `C_k`, `D_k`, and custom generator sets"},"1146":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-70","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"prefer `fnp.random.symmetric()` for synthetic data generation"},"1147":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-71","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"`fnp.random.symmetric` internally samples data and calls `flops.symmetrize` so the\nprojection and validation behavior is identical."},"1148":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-72","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"approximate costs (meaningful estimate), for the default\n`mode=\"reynolds-projection\"`:"},"1149":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-73","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"`fnp.random.symmetric`: `C_dist(n) + |G| * n + n`"},"1150":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-74","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"`flops.symmetrize`: `|G| * n + n`\\\nwith `n` total elements and `|G|` group order. The internal validation\npass is not billed. Reynolds averaging always accumulates in float64, so\n`flops.symmetrize` is charged at the float64 rate whatever the input\ndtype; `mode=\"canonical-copy\"` keeps the input's own rate."},"1151":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-75","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"in exact arithmetic it projects onto the invariant subspace, and in practice\n`flops.as_symmetric()` validates the result with its usual validation tolerances"},"1152":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-76","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"Both functions also accept `mode=\"canonical-copy\"`, which keeps one entry per\norbit — the one at the lexicographically smallest index — instead of averaging\nthe orbit. It replaces the `|G| * n + n` projection term with a single `n`\ncopy pass (so `flops.symmetrize` costs `n`, and `fnp.random.symmetric`\ncosts `C_dist(n) + n`), preserves the input dtype instead of promoting to\nfloat64, and reads only the group's generators, so it stays available for groups\ntoo large to enumerate (see below). It is a different result, not a cheaper\nroute to the same one: the other entries in each orbit are discarded rather than\naveraged in. Reach for it when you want a tensor that *has* the symmetry, and\nfor Reynolds when you want the projection of your data *onto* the invariant\nsubspace."},"1153":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-77","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"it keeps examples consistent across symmetry classes"},"1154":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-78","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#generating-example-data-with-the-reynolds-operator","content":"The propagation examples below assume `import flopscope as flops` plus `import flopscope.numpy as fnp`; generated tensors\nabove use `fnp.random.symmetric(...)` and the explicit transform helper is\n`flops.symmetrize(...)`."},"1155":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-79","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"Symmetry propagation is conservative. Flopscope keeps symmetry metadata only when\nthe operation's structure guarantees that the output still respects the\nsurviving group."},"1156":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-80","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"Operation"},"1157":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-81","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"Result type"},"1158":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-82","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"Rule"},"1159":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-83","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`fnp.exp(s3_tensor)`"},"1160":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-84","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`SymmetricTensor`"},"1161":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-85","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"unary pointwise ops preserve the exact declared group"},"1162":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-86","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`fnp.add(s3_tensor, c3_tensor)`"},"1163":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-87","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`SymmetricTensor` or `FlopscopeArray`"},"1164":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-88","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"binary pointwise ops keep the intersection of both operands' groups"},"1165":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-89","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`s2_matrix * 3`"},"1166":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-90","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`SymmetricTensor`"},"1167":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-91","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"scalar binary ops preserve the tensor's groups"},"1168":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-92","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`s2_matrix[0]`"},"1169":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-93","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`FlopscopeArray`"},"1170":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-94","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"integer indexing removes one axis; no nontrivial group survives"},"1171":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-95","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`s2_matrix[:3, :3]`"},"1172":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-96","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`SymmetricTensor`"},"1173":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-97","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"equal-size slices can preserve symmetry"},"1174":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-98","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`s2_matrix[:3, :2]`"},"1175":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-99","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`FlopscopeArray`"},"1176":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-100","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"unequal-size slices break symmetry between those axes"},"1177":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-101","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`s2_matrix[fnp.array([0, 1])]`"},"1178":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-102","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`FlopscopeArray`"},"1179":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-103","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"advanced indexing drops symmetry conservatively"},"1180":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-104","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`fnp.sum(s3_tensor, axis=0)`"},"1181":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-105","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`SymmetricTensor`"},"1182":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-106","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"reductions keep the surviving setwise stabilizer subgroup"},"1183":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-107","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`fnp.sum(d4_tensor, axis=(1, 3))`"},"1184":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-108","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`SymmetricTensor`"},"1185":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-109","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"reductions can keep a proper subgroup like `C_2`"},"1186":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-110","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`s2_matrix @ s2_matrix`"},"1187":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-111","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`FlopscopeArray`"},"1188":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-112","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"matrix products are not assumed symmetric in general"},"1189":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-113","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#symmetry-propagation-at-a-glance","content":"`fnp.exp(c3_tensor)` keeps the original `C_3` subgroup exactly; the same applies\nto `D_k` and custom exact groups."},"1190":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-114","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"Slicing uses the **pointwise stabilizer** of the removed axes. Informally:\nevery removed axis must stay fixed under any surviving group element."},"1191":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-115","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"What happens here:"},"1192":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-116","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"`same_size_slice` stays symmetric because both surviving axes still have the\nsame size"},"1193":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-117","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"`different_size_slice` loses symmetry because the two axes no longer match"},"1194":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-118","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"`expanded_s2_matrix` keeps the same `S_2` action, but the axes are renumbered\nfrom `(0, 1)` to `(1, 2)`"},"1195":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-119","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"`advanced_index_slice` returns a dense `FlopscopeArray`; flopscope does not attempt to\npropagate symmetry through array/list indexing"},"1196":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-120","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"`Ellipsis` behaves like NumPy's normal expansion rules. It can change which\naxes remain, but it does not change the propagation rule itself."},"1197":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-121","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"The difference between full and non-full groups matters immediately:"},"1198":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-122","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"`s3_slice` keeps an `S_2` subgroup on the surviving axes"},"1199":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-123","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#slicing-rules","content":"`c3_slice` loses all nontrivial symmetry, because `C_3` has no non-identity\nelement that fixes one point"},"1200":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-124","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#reduction-rules","content":"Reductions use the **setwise stabilizer** of the reduced axes. Informally:\nreduced axes are allowed to permute among themselves, because summation treats\nall positions along the reduced set equivalently."},"1201":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-125","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#reduction-rules","content":"What happens here:"},"1202":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-126","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#reduction-rules","content":"`s3_reduced` keeps an `S_2` subgroup on the remaining axes"},"1203":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-127","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#reduction-rules","content":"`c3_reduced` loses all nontrivial symmetry"},"1204":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-128","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#reduction-rules","content":"`c4_reduced` keeps a `C_2` subgroup"},"1205":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-129","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#reduction-rules","content":"`c4_keepdims` keeps the same surviving subgroup, but the output axes stay in\ntheir original tensor positions because `keepdims=True`"},"1206":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-130","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#reduction-rules","content":"Reducing an axis that is not in a symmetry group leaves that group alone, apart\nfrom any axis renumbering caused by the removed dimension."},"1207":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-131","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#binary-pointwise-ops-and-broadcasting","content":"Binary pointwise ops keep only the symmetry present in **both** operands. For\ngeneral groups, that means element-set intersection on matching output axes,\nnot just matching tuples of axis numbers."},"1208":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-132","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#binary-pointwise-ops-and-broadcasting","content":"`intersection_tensor` keeps `C_3`, because `C_3` is the common subgroup of\n`S_3` and `C_3`."},"1209":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-133","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#binary-pointwise-ops-and-broadcasting","content":"Multiple groups are handled independently:"},"1210":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-134","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#binary-pointwise-ops-and-broadcasting","content":"`shared_group_tensor` keeps only the swap on `(0, 1)`. In the exact-group\nrepresentation that surviving action is still embedded in the full output rank,\nso the group's support tuple spans `(0, 1, 2, 3)` even though it acts\nnontrivially only on `(0, 1)`."},"1211":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-135","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#binary-pointwise-ops-and-broadcasting","content":"Broadcasting matters too:"},"1212":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-136","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#binary-pointwise-ops-and-broadcasting","content":"Before group intersection, any axis stretched from size `1` to a larger output\nsize is removed from the carried candidate group. So singleton broadcasting by\nitself does not preserve symmetry. In this example, though, `plain_tensor`\nalready carries the same analytically provable `S_2` symmetry on `(0, 1)`, so\n`broadcast_sum` keeps that shared block."},"1213":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-137","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#warnings-conservative-behavior-and-re-tagging","content":"Flopscope propagates symmetry metadata conservatively. When the operation does not\nguarantee that the declared symmetry survives, the result falls back to a dense\n`FlopscopeArray` with no `.symmetry` metadata."},"1214":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-138","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#warnings-conservative-behavior-and-re-tagging","content":"`row_slice` is a plain dense `FlopscopeArray`, not a `SymmetricTensor`. That is expected."},"1215":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-139","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#warnings-conservative-behavior-and-re-tagging","content":"`SymmetryLossWarning` tells you that metadata was dropped or weakened during an\noperation. If you know more about the result than the conservative propagation\nrule does, you can re-tag it with `flops.as_symmetric()`."},"1216":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-140","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#warnings-conservative-behavior-and-re-tagging","content":"One important caveat: the current implementation does **not** report every\npossible partial weakening via `SymmetryLossWarning`. Treat the warning system\nas helpful guidance, not as a complete audit of every same-axis subgroup\nchange."},"1217":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-141","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#edge-cases-when-symmetrictensors-meet-numpy-protocols","content":"Once your tensors flow through arbitrary user code (or third-party libraries), they\ninevitably hit NumPy's `__array_ufunc__` and `__array_function__` machinery —\n`np.add(A, B)`, `np.divmod(A, B)`, `np.add.outer(A, B)`, `np.add.at(A, idx, vals)`,\n`A @= B`, `np.tensordot(A, B, axes=...)`, and so on. Flopscope's\nprotocol implementations are conservative: when an operation would silently\ncorrupt your declared symmetry, flopscope **refuses or strips** rather than letting\nit through. This section is a tour of those edge cases."},"1218":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-142","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#in-place-dunders-refuse-symmetry-corrupting-writes","content":"The right-hand-side has no symmetry, so `A_sym + B_plain` returns a plain\n`FlopscopeArray`. Writing that result back into `A_sym`'s buffer would leave the\nmetadata claiming symmetry while the data is asymmetric. Flopscope refuses with\n`ValueError: in-place add on a SymmetricTensor would weaken or destroy the\ndeclared symmetry`."},"1219":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-143","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#in-place-dunders-refuse-symmetry-corrupting-writes","content":"If you know the asymmetric write is intentional, downgrade first:"},"1220":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-144","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#in-place-dunders-refuse-symmetry-corrupting-writes","content":"The same guard applies to `__isub__`, `__imul__`, `__itruediv__`,\n`__ifloordiv__`, `__imod__`, `__ipow__`, `__iand__`, `__ior__`, `__ixor__`,\n`__ilshift__`, `__irshift__`, and `__imatmul__` (which additionally falls back\nto CPython's rebind-the-name semantics when the matmul output shape differs\nfrom `self.shape`)."},"1221":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-145","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#in-place-sort--partition-refuse-on-symmetrictensor","content":"A reorder along any axis breaks the permutation invariance. Use the out-of-place\nforms instead:"},"1222":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-146","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#ufuncat-refuses-on-symmetrictensor","content":"`np.add.at(a, indices, values)` does an unbuffered fancy-index write — every\nrepeat of an index applies again (unlike `a[indices] += values` which dedupes).\nOn a `SymmetricTensor` this almost certainly breaks symmetry, so flopscope\nrefuses:"},"1223":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-147","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#ufuncat-refuses-on-symmetrictensor","content":"Downgrade with `A_sym.view(fnp.ndarray)` first if you really need the unbuffered\nupdate."},"1224":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-148","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#ufuncouter-produces-direct-product-symmetry","content":"When both operands are symmetric, the output of `np.<ufunc>.outer(A, B)`\ninherits the **direct product** of the input symmetries — `A`'s symmetry on\nits own axes, `B`'s symmetry on the lifted slots `A.ndim..A.ndim+B.ndim-1`:"},"1225":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-149","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#tensordot-keeps-surviving-direct-product-symmetry","content":"The contracted axes drop out of each operand's symmetry; what's left of each\noperand's symmetry on the surviving (uncontracted) axes is direct-producted:"},"1226":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-150","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#tensordot-keeps-surviving-direct-product-symmetry","content":"If the contracted axis is part of the symmetry group, that group is destroyed\non the corresponding side."},"1227":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-151","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#multi-output-ufuncs-preserve-symmetry-on-every-output","content":"`np.divmod(A, B)`, `np.frexp(A)`, `np.modf(A)` are elementwise — both outputs\ninherit the same symmetry as their inputs:"},"1228":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-152","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#multi-output-ufuncs-preserve-symmetry-on-every-output","content":"`out=(o1, o2)` works (per-slot identity is preserved), and `out=(o1, None)`\nlets numpy allocate just the second slot."},"1229":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-153","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#cost-model-is-a-placeholder-above-the-dimino_budget-group-order","content":"Flopscope charges `dense_cost × unique_output_elements / dense_output_elements` for\n`ufunc.outer` and `tensordot` as a coarse proxy for the savings a\nsymmetry-aware implementation could realise. The underlying NumPy call still\ndoes dense work; only the budget reflects the savings."},"1230":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-154","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#cost-model-is-a-placeholder-above-the-dimino_budget-group-order","content":"For a `SymmetryGroup` whose order `|G|` exceeds the configured `dimino_budget`\n(default **50,000** — accepts up to `|S_8| = 40,320` with a small margin),\nflopscope skips this adjustment and charges the full dense cost. Burnside\nenumeration on the group would otherwise iterate every element. The skip is\nannounced via `CostFallbackWarning`:"},"1231":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-155","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#cost-model-is-a-placeholder-above-the-dimino_budget-group-order","content":"This is rare in practice — common user tensors have small group orders — but\nhigh-rank auto-inferred symmetries on degenerate shapes (`(1,)*N` for large `N`)\ntrip the cap. The warning fires once per `(op_name, |G|)` pair per process to\navoid log flooding. Suppress with `flops.configure(symmetry_warnings=False)`,\nwhich shares the flag with `SymmetryLossWarning`."},"1232":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-156","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#cost-model-is-a-placeholder-above-the-dimino_budget-group-order","content":"`flops.symmetrize` and `fnp.random.symmetric` are the exception to the\ndegrade-and-continue behaviour above: for them, enumerating the group *is* the\ncomputation, so there is no dense cost to fall back to. Above the budget they\nraise `ValueError` rather than warn, and the refused call is not charged. Use\n`mode=\"canonical-copy\"`, which reads the generators alone and never enumerates\nthe group:"},"1233":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-157","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#cost-model-is-a-placeholder-above-the-dimino_budget-group-order","content":"The gate keys on `|G|` rather than degree because `|G|` is the actual driver\nof enumeration cost. A high-degree but small-order group like\n`SymmetryGroup.cyclic(axes=tuple(range(50)))` (degree 50, `|G| = 50`) passes\nthe gate easily, whereas `SymmetryGroup.symmetric(axes=tuple(range(9)))`\n(degree 9, `|G| = 362,880`) bails. For known group families\n(`symmetric`, `cyclic`, `dihedral`, `young`, `direct_product`) `order()` is\nclosed-form O(1), so the check itself never enumerates. Tune the budget with\n`flops.configure(dimino_budget=...)`, or run\n`python -m benchmarks._perm_group_calibration` for a recommendation tied to\nyour host machine."},"1234":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-158","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#cost-model-is-a-placeholder-above-the-dimino_budget-group-order","content":"A proper algorithmic-cost model is a follow-up."},"1235":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-159","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"The propagation rules are easier to predict if you keep three ideas in mind:"},"1236":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-160","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"**Unary pointwise ops** preserve symmetry-aware costs, but for\nfull and non-full groups alike they keep the same exact group"},"1237":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-161","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"**Slicing** uses the pointwise stabilizer of the removed axes"},"1238":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-162","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"**Reductions** use the setwise stabilizer of the reduced axes"},"1239":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-163","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"**Binary pointwise ops** use intersection of both operands' groups after\nbroadcast alignment"},"1240":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-164","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"After computing the surviving subgroup, flopscope restricts it to the axes still\npresent in the output and remaps those axes to the output tensor's numbering."},"1241":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-165","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"That is why:"},"1242":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-166","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"slicing one axis of `S_3` leaves `S_2`"},"1243":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-167","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"slicing one axis of `C_3` leaves nothing nontrivial"},"1244":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-168","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#under-the-hood","content":"reducing `{1, 3}` of `C_4` can still leave `C_2`"},"1245":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-169","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#going-deeper","content":"Einsum Patterns — how declared and induced symmetry interact with `fnp.einsum`"},"1246":{"id":"/docs/guides/symmetry-170","page_id":"/docs/guides/symmetry","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/symmetry#going-deeper","content":"Symmetry Detection Deep Dive — the full detection algorithm for `einsum`"},"1247":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"page","content":"FLOP Counting Model","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Understanding Flopscope"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model"},"1248":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-0","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","content":"How Flopscope bills compute: the cost model, by family rule."},"1249":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#cost-model-reference","content":"Cost model reference"},"1250":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-2","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#how-to-read-this","content":"How to read this"},"1251":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-3","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"Billing model & design principles"},"1252":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-4","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fma2","content":"FMA=2"},"1253":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-5","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#comparison-and-select","content":"Comparison and select"},"1254":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-6","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#transcendental-tier-weight-160","content":"Transcendental tier (weight 16.0)"},"1255":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-7","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#access-tier-weight-40","content":"Access tier (weight 4.0)"},"1256":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-8","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"The unifying philosophy — every byte written is metered"},"1257":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-9","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#views-and-metadata-weight-00","content":"Views and metadata (weight 0.0)"},"1258":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-10","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#composite-ops-weight-10-with-heterogeneous-flop_cost","content":"Composite ops (weight 1.0 with heterogeneous flop_cost)"},"1259":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-11","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#numpy-2x-ufunc-aliases","content":"NumPy 2.x ufunc aliases"},"1260":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-12","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#dtype-and-precision","content":"Dtype and precision"},"1261":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-13","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-countin