:"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#set-ops","content":"Set ops"},"1287":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-39","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#counting-diff-ediff1d-clip-allclose-isclose-count_nonzero-trace","content":"Counting (diff, ediff1d, clip, allclose, isclose, count_nonzero, trace)"},"1288":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-40","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather","content":"Copy and gather"},"1289":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-41","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather-ops-with-distinct-charged-siblings","content":"Copy-and-gather: ops with distinct charged siblings"},"1290":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-42","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#index-generators","content":"Index generators"},"1291":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-43","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#boundary-ops-free-behavior--a-value-computing-path","content":"Boundary ops (free behavior + a value-computing path)"},"1292":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-44","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#io-save--load","content":"I/O (save / load)"},"1293":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-45","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#functional--higher-order","content":"Functional / higher-order"},"1294":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-46","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#view--free-weight-00","content":"View / free (weight 0.0)"},"1295":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-47","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#exhaustive-per-op-reference","content":"Exhaustive per-op reference"},"1296":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-48","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#cost-model-reference","content":"> **Start here.** This is the cost model's conceptual and audit reference. Read it\n> to understand *how* billing works and audit its accounting safeguards — you do\n> **not** need to read every operation. The exhaustive,\n> generated per-op list (every op with its `cost_formula` and `weight`) lives in\n> `ops.json` and the website API pages; this doc\n> explains the model by **family rule** so you can reason about a whole class at once."},"1297":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-49","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#cost-model-reference","content":"flopscope bills compute as:"},"1298":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-50","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#how-to-read-this","content":"**Billing model & design principles** — the one equation and *why* it is split into four factors."},"1299":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-51","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#how-to-read-this","content":"**Dtype and precision** — how width (`dtype_rate`) and complex structure (`complex_factor`) are priced, and why."},"1300":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-52","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#how-to-read-this","content":"**Non-exploitability** — the invariants that keep billing sound, and the test that enforces each."},"1301":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-53","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#how-to-read-this","content":"**Cost by family** — the rule + evidence + representative ops for the family you care about."},"1302":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-54","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#how-to-read-this","content":"**Exhaustive per-op reference** — drill into `ops.json` for one op's exact formula."},"1303":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-55","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#how-to-read-this","content":"**Completeness guarantee:** every billed operation is classified in the registry and\nappears in `ops.json` with a `cost_formula`; `tests/test_cost_model_coverage.py`\nenforces both that, and that every op-class (`ops.json` *area*) is covered by a family\nrule below. So nothing billed is undocumented, even where this doc summarizes by rule."},"1304":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-56","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"Every operation is charged `charged = int(flop_cost × dtype_rate × complex_factor × weight)`."},"1305":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-57","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"**Four factors, on purpose.** Each factor isolates one kind of reasoning so it can be\nread off and audited on its own:"},"1306":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-58","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"**`flop_cost`** — the operation count. Every shape- and algorithm-dependent term lives\nhere, so anyone — us or a participant — can read it off the formula and audit it\nfunction by function."},"1307":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-59","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"**`dtype_rate`** — the width factor. One billed FLOP is one 32-bit-class real\noperation; a 64-bit-class operand bills `2×`. Like the weight ladder this is a *policy*\ntable (subject to tuning), not shape math — defined in\nDtype and precision."},"1308":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-60","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"**`complex_factor`** — the complex-structure factor. It is `1` for real dtypes and, for\ncomplex dtypes, the number of real (component-precision) operations one billed unit\nexpands into (a complex multiply is 6 real FLOPs, a complex add 2). It is *math*, not\npolicy — a textbook decomposition fixed per op, and computed per call for the\ncontraction family from the same accumulation that produces `flop_cost`."},"1309":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-61","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"**`weight`** — the hardware tier. A separate per-element factor for how much more one\nelement costs on real hardware: a plain sequential add or write is `1`, a\nnon-sequential memory access (a sort's comparison order, a computed-index gather, a\nrandom-reorder draw) is `4`, and a transcendental element (`sin`, `exp`, …) does many\ntimes more floating-point work at `16`. Rather than bill each op its exact measured\nratio — noisy, machine-specific, and hard to audit — we group operations into a small\nfixed ladder of tiers (`{0, 1, 4, 16}`); that grouping is a deliberate\ncompetition-design choice."},"1310":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-62","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"For a real 32-bit workload `dtype_rate` and `complex_factor` are both `1`, so the bill is\nexactly `int(flop_cost × weight)` and nothing here changes."},"1311":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-63","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"The rule that keeps these factors separate is one **separation\ninvariant**: *a shape or algorithm constant never lives in a weight* (anything depending\non a matrix dimension or loop length belongs in `flop_cost`); &#x2A;width policy never lives in\n`flop_cost` or `weight`* (it lives in `dtype_rate`); and &#x2A;complex structure lives in\n`complex_factor`* — computed per call for the contraction family from the same\ndecomposition that produces `flop_cost`. (The weight half is enforced by\n`tests/test_weight_tier_policy.py`; the complex-classification half by\n`tests/test_complex_factor_completeness.py`.)"},"1312":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-64","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"**We bill the textbook standard-algorithm cost, not literal BLAS/LAPACK.**\n`linalg.inv` is billed `2n³` (the standard LU-based `dgetrf`+`dgetri` operation\ncount) regardless of what the underlying library does; top-k SVD is billed as the\nstandard truncated-algorithm cost. This keeps billing deterministic,\nhardware-independent, and composable."},"1313":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-65","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#billing-model--design-principles","content":"The rest of this section defines the conventions these principles rest on."},"1314":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-66","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fma2","content":"Each floating-point multiply, add, subtract, divide, or square root counts\nas 1 FLOP.  A fused multiply-add (FMA) therefore counts as 2.  This matches\nthe standard textbook convention.  All formulas in this document are stated\nin FMA=2 units unless noted."},"1315":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-67","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fma2","content":"All formulas count *operations*; the billing **unit** (one 32-bit-class real\nop) and the per-width and complex-structure pricing are defined once in\nDtype and precision."},"1316":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-68","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#comparison-and-select","content":"A single comparison (`>`, `==`, `!=`, …) or conditional-select (`where`,\n`choose`) counts as 1 FLOP.  Sorting, partition, and percentile operations\nuse this convention when counting per-element work."},"1317":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-69","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#transcendental-tier-weight-160","content":"Operations whose per-element cost is dominated by a libm minimax polynomial\nevaluation (sin, cos, tan, exp, log, arcsin, arccos, arctan, arcsinh,\narccosh, arctanh, power, and their NumPy 2.x aliases) are billed at weight\n16.0.  The `flop_cost` formula is `numel(output)` (1 per element); the 16×\nfactor is supplied entirely by the weight."},"1318":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-70","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#transcendental-tier-weight-160","content":"A subset of moderate-cost binary ops (floor\\_divide, mod/remainder, fmod,\narctan2, hypot, logaddexp, logaddexp2) is grouped into the same tier\n(weight 16.0)."},"1319":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-71","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#access-tier-weight-40","content":"Operations whose per-element cost is dominated by a **non-sequential memory access** —\ncomparison-sort order, binary search, a computed-index gather, or a random-reorder draw\n— are billed at weight 4.0. `flop_cost` stays the plain algorithmic count (comparisons,\nindex dereferences, or draws); the 4× comes entirely from the weight, the same\none-factor-does-one-job split the transcendental tier uses."},"1320":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-72","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#access-tier-weight-40","content":"This tier covers four families, each detailed in its own section below:"},"1321":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-73","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#access-tier-weight-40","content":"**Sort / search** — `sort`, `argsort`, `lexsort`, `partition`, `argpartition`,\n`searchsorted` (see Sort and select)."},"1322":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-74","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#access-tier-weight-40","content":"**Set / histogram** — `unique` and its NumPy 2.x siblings, `union1d`, `intersect1d`,\n`setdiff1d`, `setxor1d`, `histogram`, `histogram2d`, `histogramdd`,\n`histogram_bin_edges`, `bincount`, `digitize` (see Set ops and Interp and\nhistogram)."},"1323":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-75","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#access-tier-weight-40","content":"**Gather** — `take`, `take_along_axis`, `choose`: a computed-index read is a\nnon-sequential memory access, unlike a materializing copy's sequential write (see\nCopy and gather)."},"1324":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-76","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#access-tier-weight-40","content":"**Random reorder** — `random.permutation`, `random.shuffle`, `random.choice`, and the\n`Generator`/`RandomState` `.choice` / `.permutation` / `.shuffle` (plus `Generator.permuted`)\nmethods (see Random). Note\n`random.sample` is *not* in this tier — despite the name it is numpy's alias for\n`random_sample`, a plain uniform draw."},"1325":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-77","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#access-tier-weight-40","content":"A materializing copy or write (`concatenate`, `ones`, a scatter write) stays at weight\n1.0 — it touches memory sequentially, one write per element. The access tier prices the\n*pattern* of access, not merely the presence of a write."},"1326":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-78","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"> **flopscope meters both computation on values and the memory traffic that produces\n> them.** A **view** is free — it creates no new buffer and touches no element. Any op\n> that **writes a new buffer** is charged at least `1` per element written, whether the\n> values it writes are computed (`sin(x)`), copied (`concatenate`), replicated\n> (`tile`), or a repeated constant (`ones`). Ops whose access pattern is\n> **non-sequential** — sorting, a computed-index gather, a random-reorder draw — are\n> charged more per element (`4`) than a straight sequential write (`1`), because that\n> is where real hardware actually spends more per element: cache-friendly sequential\n> writes are cheap, branchy/random-order accesses are not."},"1327":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-79","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"**The decision procedure** — apply these steps in order to any op:"},"1328":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-80","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"**View / metadata only** (returns a view or read-only reinterpretation of existing\nmemory, inspects shape/dtype, allocates no new buffer)? → &#x2A;*Free (0).** Reshape,\nravel, and `copy` do **not** qualify even when NumPy happens to return a view —\nflopscope bills them as if they always materialize, so the price cannot depend on a\nlayout coincidence (see Views and metadata)."},"1329":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-81","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"**Does it write a new buffer sequentially** — a materializing copy (`concatenate`,\n`tile`, `roll`), a constant fill that isn't the zero-page default (`ones`, `full`,\n`eye`'s diagonal), a scatter write at a *given* index (`put`, `putmask`,\n`fill_diagonal`), or output-shaped selection (`select`, `compress`, `extract`)? →\n&#x2A;*Charged at weight 1.0.** `flop_cost` is the count of elements actually written\n(not the whole array when only part of it changes, e.g. `eye`'s off-diagonal zeros\nare free)."},"1330":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-82","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"**Does it read via a *non-sequential* access pattern** — a computed-index gather\n(`take`, `take_along_axis`, `choose`), a comparison-order derivation (`sort`,\n`unique`, `searchsorted`), a per-element conditional select (`where(cond, x, y)`,\nwhich dereferences a different source per output element and bills\n`4 × numel(broadcast output)`), or a random-reorder draw (`shuffle`, `permutation`,\n`choice`)? → **Charged at weight 4.0** (see Access tier)."},"1331":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-83","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"**Does it produce output values by doing floating-point arithmetic, *or* by\ncomparing element values?** → &#x2A;*Charged.** `flop_cost` = standard-algorithm op\ncount; `weight` = hardware tier. This includes elementwise math, transcendentals,\nreductions, contraction (matmul/einsum), FFT, polynomial, random generation, and\nops that *derive* a result by *testing values*: `nonzero`/`argwhere`/\n`flatnonzero`/`count_nonzero`/`where(1-arg)`, `clip`/`minimum`/`maximum`, and\n*computed creators* (`arange`/`linspace`/`geomspace`/`logspace`/`vander`)."},"1332":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-84","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"**Otherwise** — allocates a new buffer but writes nothing into it (`zeros`,\n`empty`) → &#x2A;*Free (0).** The OS zero-page (or uninitialized allocation) costs\nnothing to hand out; a participant only pays once real values land in it."},"1333":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-85","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"**Key invariant:** any predicate or index feeding a step-2/3 op was itself produced by\nan earlier charged step. A free-tier op may **never bundle** value-arithmetic,\nvalue-comparison, or a non-zero write into its own cost."},"1334":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-86","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-unifying-philosophy--every-byte-written-is-metered","content":"The active weights are `{0, 1, 4, 16}`. Weight 0 is now a narrow band — genuine views,\nmetadata, and all-zero/uninitialized allocation. Almost everything that writes a new\nbuffer bills at least weight 1.0; the historical \"free data movement\" framing (copy,\ngather, scatter, and select-with-a-given-selector all billed 0) has been replaced by\nthis write-metered model, since a participant who can move arbitrary amounts of data\nfor free can launder real compute through a materializing copy chain."},"1335":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-87","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#views-and-metadata-weight-00","content":"Weight 0 is now a narrow band — a new buffer is never allocated, or it is allocated\nbut nothing is written into it:"},"1336":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-88","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#views-and-metadata-weight-00","content":"**Views / metadata** — operations that return a view of existing memory or inspect\nmetadata without touching element values: `transpose`, `swapaxes`,\n`moveaxis`, `squeeze`, `expand_dims`, `flip`/`fliplr`/`flipud`, `rot90`,\n`atleast_1d`/`atleast_2d`/`atleast_3d`, `broadcast_to`, `view`,\n`real`/`imag` (component extraction), `split`, `hsplit`, `vsplit`,\n`array_split`, `unstack`, `diagonal` (the 2-D view path — see\nCopy-and-gather for the\n1-D *construct* path, which writes), `linalg.diagonal`, `linalg.matrix_transpose`,\n`from_dlpack` (zero-copy ingest), and all other shape/stride/dtype introspection\n(`ndim`, `shape`, `size`, `nbytes`, `itemsize`, `dtype`, `flags`, `base`, `data`,\n`ctypes`, `strides`, `T`, `isscalar`, `isfortran&#x60;).\n**`reshape`, `ravel`, `copy`, `flatten`, and `require` do *not* belong here** —\nflopscope bills them `numel(input)` at weight 1.0 unconditionally, even on the\n(common) call pattern where NumPy itself returns a view. Billing the cautious,\nalways-charged price avoids a participant relying on a layout coincidence\n(contiguity, stride pattern) to get a real copy for free. `flatten` is not even a\ncoincidence: `ndarray.flatten` is documented by NumPy to always allocate a new\nbuffer, never a view."},"1337":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-89","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#views-and-metadata-weight-00","content":"**All-zero / uninitialized allocation** — a new buffer whose contents are the\nplatform zero-page default, so nothing is actually written: `zeros`, `zeros_like`,\n`empty`, `empty_like`. Any *other* constant fill (`ones`, `full`, `eye`'s diagonal,\n`identity`, `tri`) writes real, non-zero values and is charged — see\nCopy and gather."},"1338":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-90","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#views-and-metadata-weight-00","content":"**Refinement — representation vs. value change** (resolves `astype`/`asarray`):"},"1339":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-91","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#views-and-metadata-weight-00","content":"> **A representation change that performs no work is free; every cast or copy that\n> actually runs is charged the same as `copy`.**"},"1340":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-92","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#views-and-metadata-weight-00","content":"`astype` and `asarray` bill `numel(input)` at the heavier of the source/destination\ndtype rate — the same formula `copy` bills (see Which dtype prices a\ncall) — for every call that does real work: narrowing,\nwidening, float→int truncation, bool coercion, a same-dtype `astype` called with the\ndefault `copy=True`, and any `asarray(x, dtype=other)` that actually converts the\nbuffer. The one free case left is the genuine no-op, where NumPy itself performs zero\nwork: `astype(x, dtype, copy=False)` when `dtype` already equals `x`'s dtype returns\nthe *identical object*, and `asarray(x)` with no `dtype=` (or a `dtype=` that already\nmatches `x`'s dtype) performs no conversion — both bill `0`, same as the views/metadata\nops above. Any other combination — `copy=False` across an actual dtype change (NumPy\ncannot honor the request and copies anyway), or any other `astype`/`asarray` call —\nperforms a real `numel`-element write and is charged."},"1341":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-93","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#views-and-metadata-weight-00","content":"This closes a loophole where `x.astype(x.dtype)` (default `copy=True`) was a free\nsubstitute for `x.copy()`, and `x.astype(bool)` / `x.astype(int)` were free substitutes\nfor a billed `!= 0` test or a `trunc`-then-cast. It also means choosing a narrower\nworking dtype now costs a one-time `copy`-priced toll to get there; the arithmetic\nperformed *afterward* on the narrower array is still cheaper per the dtype-rate table\n(see Which dtype prices a call), so that saving is not\neliminated — only no longer free to acquire up front. Contrast the charged cases with\n`ones`/`full`, which also touch every element but write an actual value into memory —\nthose are charged the same way (see Views and metadata\ncategory 2 above and Copy and gather)."},"1342":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-94","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#views-and-metadata-weight-00","content":"The method `a.nonzero()` is charged identically to `fnp.nonzero(a)`; `nonzero`,\n`argwhere`, `flatnonzero`, `count_nonzero`, and `where` (1-arg) remain charged\n`numel(input)` at weight 1.0 — they derive their result by testing element values\n(`!= 0`), and that test is real compute regardless of how \"small\" the eventual output\nis (see Copy and gather for the full selector-family accounting)."},"1343":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-95","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#composite-ops-weight-10-with-heterogeneous-flop_cost","content":"When an operation mixes sub-tiers internally (e.g. random samplers, stats\nkernels, norms with SVD), all per-element factors are folded into `flop_cost`\nand the active weight is set to 1.0.  This avoids double-counting with the\ntier factor."},"1344":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-96","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#numpy-2x-ufunc-aliases","content":"NumPy 2.x introduced `acos`, `acosh`, `asin`, `asinh`, `atan`, `atanh`, `atan2`, and\n`pow` as canonical aliases for their `arc*` / `power` counterparts — literally the same\nufunc object (`np.acos is np.arccos`). flopscope resolves these via\n`_UFUNC_ALIAS_RENAMES` in `_weights.py` so each alias charges the same **weight** as its\ncanonical twin."},"1345":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-97","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#numpy-2x-ufunc-aliases","content":"`divmod` rides the same rename map but is not a true 1:1 alias: `np.divmod` is a distinct\ntwo-output ufunc (`nin=2, nout=2`) from `np.floor_divide` (`nin=2, nout=1`) —\n`np.divmod is np.floor_divide` is `False`. `_UFUNC_ALIAS_RENAMES` only borrows\n`floor_divide`'s **weight** (16.0) for `divmod` as a conservative floor; `divmod`'s\n`flop_cost` is its own `nout=2 × numel(output)` (see\nElementwise), so it bills 2× `floor_divide`'s\ntotal, not the same total."},"1346":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-98","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#dtype-and-precision","content":"`flop_cost` counts operations, but two operations of the same shape can do very different\namounts of real floating-point work depending on their **dtype**. Two factors price that:\n`dtype_rate` (how wide each scalar is) and `complex_factor` (how much a complex operation\nexpands into real ones). Both sit in the billing product\n`int(flop_cost × dtype_rate × complex_factor × weight)`, and both are `1.0` for the\n32-bit real baseline — so a real-fp32 workload bills exactly its `flop_cost × weight` and\nnothing in this section changes it."},"1347":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-99","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#why-width-and-structure-are-priced","content":"We identified two ways a dtype-blind meter under-counts:"},"1348":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-100","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#why-width-and-structure-are-priced","content":"**Complex packing.** A complex multiply does the work of four real multiplies and two\nreal adds, but a dtype-blind meter would bill `x * z` (with `z` complex) as one real\nmultiply. A participant can then carry two independent real payloads in the real and\nimaginary lanes of one complex array and recover both products from a single \"one-FLOP\"\nmultiply — buying two real results for less than the price of one. The same trick scales\nthrough every complex-valued op, most sharply through matmul, where one complex matmul\nstands in for two real ones."},"1349":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-101","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#why-width-and-structure-are-priced","content":"**Width (bit) packing.** A float64 lane is twice as wide as a float32 lane. A dtype-blind\nmeter bills a float64 multiply the same as a float32 multiply, so a participant can pack\ntwo independent lower-precision payloads into the two halves of a 64-bit lane (or a narrow\ninteger into a wide one) and process both under a single op's charge."},"1350":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-102","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#why-width-and-structure-are-priced","content":"The meter addresses these cases by pricing width and complex structure.\n`dtype_rate` charges a 64-bit-class op `2×` a 32-bit-class op;\n`complex_factor` charges a complex op the real-operation count it actually expands into.\nThese prices address the examples below; they do not guarantee that every packing\nstrategy is unprofitable or permitted. Competition eligibility is governed separately\nby the official Rules (see On packing)."},"1351":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-103","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"> **One billed FLOP is one 32-bit-class real scalar operation** — a float32 or int32 add,\n> multiply, compare, and so on."},"1352":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-104","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"Everything is priced relative to that anchor. `dtype_rate` is the width multiplier for the\ncall's resolved dtype:"},"1353":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-105","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"dtype"},"1354":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-106","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"rate"},"1355":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-107","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"dtype"},"1356":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-108","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"rate"},"1357":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-109","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`bool`"},"1358":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-110","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"1.0"},"1359":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-111","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`int32`"},"1360":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-112","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"1.0"},"1361":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-113","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`int8`"},"1362":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-114","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"1.0"},"1363":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-115","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`uint32`"},"1364":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-116","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"1.0"},"1365":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-117","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`uint8`"},"1366":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-118","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"1.0"},"1367":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-119","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`float32`"},"1368":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-120","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"1.0"},"1369":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-121","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`int16`"},"1370":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-122","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"1.0"},"1371":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-123","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`int64`"},"1372":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-124","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"2.0"},"1373":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-125","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`uint16`"},"1374":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-126","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"1.0"},"1375":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-127","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`uint64`"},"1376":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-128","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"2.0"},"1377":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-129","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`float16`"},"1378":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-130","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"1.0"},"1379":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-131","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`float64`"},"1380":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-132","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"2.0"},"1381":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-133","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`float96`"},"1382":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-134","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"3.0"},"1383":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-135","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`complex64`"},"1384":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-136","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"1.0"},"1385":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-137","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`float128`"},"1386":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-138","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"4.0"},"1387":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-139","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`complex128`"},"1388":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-140","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"2.0"},"1389":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-141","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`complex192`"},"1390":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-142","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"3.0"},"1391":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-143","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"`complex256`"},"1392":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-144","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"4.0"},"1393":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-145","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"**32-bit-class and narrower → `1.0`.** float16/int16/int8/bool all bill at the baseline\nwidth."},"1394":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-146","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"**64-bit-class → `2.0`.** float64, int64, uint64 do twice the scalar work of the\nbaseline width."},"1395":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-147","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"**Extended precision → width class.** `float96` (3.0) and `float128` (4.0) are the\nplatform `longdouble` types (Linux only; on macOS/Windows `longdouble` aliases\n`float64`). At these rates, packing narrower payloads through their wider mantissas\nstill loses: two float32-payload products in one `float128` multiply cost `4.0`\nagainst the honest `2 × 1.0`. Note these dtypes cannot cross the evaluation wire\n(the transfer codec accepts the 14 standard dtypes) — they are priced for in-process\ncompute only."},"1396":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-148","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"**complex → its component width's rate.** `complex64` is two float32 components → `1.0`;\n`complex128` is two float64 components → `2.0`; `complex192`/`complex256` follow their\nextended components (3.0 / 4.0). The *structure* cost of being complex (that a complex\nmultiply is several real ones) is carried separately by `complex_factor`, not folded\nin here."},"1397":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-149","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"These are **current policy values, subject to tuning** — the same status as the weight\nladder, and configured in the same file (`src/flopscope/data/default_weights.json`, under\n`\"dtype_rates\"`). They are a deliberate competition-design choice, not a hardware\nmeasurement."},"1398":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-150","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"**Fail-closed for unknown numeric dtypes.** Any *numeric* dtype not in the table (a\nfuture type numpy or an extension package might introduce) has no defined rate, so\nbilling **raises `UnsupportedDtypeError` before charging any FLOPs** rather than\nguessing — a numeric dtype cannot slip through unpriced; supporting a new one is a\ndeliberate one-line policy addition to the table. *Non-numeric* dtypes (`object`,\n`str_`, `bytes_`, `datetime64`, `timedelta64`, structured/void, object-free or not)\nare refused outright rather than priced at any rate — see the note directly below."},"1399":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-151","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-billing-unit-and-rate-table","content":"> **Non-numeric dtypes are not supported.** flopscope bills only the numeric\n> kinds — bool, signed/unsigned integer, float, complex (`dtype.kind in\n> \"biufc\"`) — a NUMERIC ALLOWLIST rather than a denylist of the non-numeric\n> kinds, so a dtype kind flopscope has never seen (a future numpy or\n> extension-package type) is refused by default instead of silently priced.\n> Two distinct dtype families fall outside it. `object` (and any\n> structured/void dtype embedding an object field — this subsumes the\n> `numpy.dtype.hasobject` check) can hold unbounded per-element computation\n> that no rate expresses. `str_`/`bytes_`/`datetime64`/`timedelta64`/\n> structured-void — object-free included — are bounded, but their real\n> per-element cost is not the fixed unit a flat rate would have to assume: a\n> wide string or record does more work than a narrow one, and\n> datetime64/timedelta64 are integers underneath, at whatever the platform's\n> integer rate is, not a dtype-blind flat one. Both are refused the same\n> way, whenever the dtype reaches a counted operation as an operand (an\n> ndarray, or a Python sequence NumPy would coerce into one), an explicit\n> `dtype=`, a fill value or distribution parameter about to be cast the same\n> way, or an `out=` destination — flopscope raises `UnsupportedDtypeError`\n> instead of billing it, including flopscope's own conversion ops\n> (`array`/`asarray`/`astype`/`fromiter`/`require`/`full`/`full_like`) and\n> every random sampler, which refuse non-numeric input rather than convert\n> or relocate it. A raw Python sequence is judged by its leaves: `str`,\n> `bytes`, a NumPy scalar whose own dtype is non-numeric, and a stdlib\n> `date`/`time`/`timedelta` are refused wherever they appear inside a\n> list/tuple operand, so `fnp.random.choice(['a','b','c','d'], 2)` is refused\n> exactly like the `np.array(['a','b','c','d'])` spelling of it. A bare\n> string ARGUMENT is untouched — `ord='fro'`, an einsum subscript, a\n> `casting=` mode and a `requirements=`/`order=`/`signature=`/`optimize=`\n> specifier are options, not payloads. One exception on the dtype side: a dtype that is\n> still zero-itemsize once NumPy *materialises* it (an empty structured spec,\n> or `'V0'`) is let through regardless of kind — zero bytes per element\n> cannot embed an object field or carry any itemsize-dependent cost, and\n> NumPy's own internals allocate one as a zero-byte shape-computation\n> placeholder. A zero-length *string* dtype is not in that exception: NumPy\n> promotes `'U0'`/`'S0'` to `'U1'`/`'S1'` on allocation, so it is refused\n> like any other string dtype. To work with mixed, ragged, or\n> non-numeric data, convert with plain NumPy *before* it reaches flopscope —\n> `clean = np.array(x, dtype=np.float64)`, **not** `fnp.array(...)`, since\n> flopscope's own conversion ops refuse non-numeric input too — or hold it\n> in a Python list of numeric arrays and process each one separately."},"1400":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-152","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"`complex_factor` answers one question per op: &#x2A;when the resolved dtype is complex, how many\nreal (component-precision) FLOPs does one billed unit of this op actually perform?* It is\n`1.0` for real dtypes. For complex dtypes it is a fixed decomposition of the op into real\narithmetic (`z = a + bi`, FMA=2 convention):"},"1401":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-153","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"atom"},"1402":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-154","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"real FLOPs"},"1403":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-155","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"decomposition"},"1404":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-156","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"add / subtract / negate"},"1405":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-157","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"2"},"1406":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-158","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"two real adds"},"1407":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-159","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"multiply"},"1408":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-160","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"6"},"1409":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-161","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"4 real mul + 2 real add"},"1410":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-162","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"divide"},"1411":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-163","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"11"},"1412":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-164","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"numerator 4 mul + 2 add; denominator 2 mul + 1 add; 2 divides"},"1413":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-165","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"reciprocal"},"1414":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-166","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"6"},"1415":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-167","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"special case of divide"},"1416":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-168","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"fused multiply-add"},"1417":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-169","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"4"},"1418":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-170","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"8 real FLOPs per FMA=2 unit (1 complex mul + 1 complex add)"},"1419":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-171","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"absolute"},"1420":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-172","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"4"},"1421":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-173","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"abs(z) = sqrt(re² + im²): 2 mul + 1 add + 1 sqrt"},"1422":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-174","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"sqrt"},"1423":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-175","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"10"},"1424":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-176","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"complex square root"},"1425":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-177","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"ordering compare"},"1426":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-178","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"2"},"1427":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-179","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"lexicographic: compare real parts, tie-break on imaginary"},"1428":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-180","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"Every charged op is classified by the atom its **billed unit** reduces to:"},"1429":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-181","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"class"},"1430":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-182","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"factor"},"1431":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-183","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"representative ops"},"1432":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-184","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"add / compare / sort / set / reduce-sum"},"1433":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-185","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**2**"},"1434":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-186","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"two real adds, two lexicographic compares, or two component relocations — a complex value is two real components, and every op prices at least one unit per component: `add`, `subtract`, `sum`, `mean`, `cumsum`, `sort`, `unique`, `where`, comparisons (`less`/`greater`/`equal`), set ops, `conj`, `angle`, `concatenate`, `take`, `random.shuffle`, and the wider copy/gather/creation family"},"1435":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-187","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"multiply / pure product"},"1436":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-188","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**6**"},"1437":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-189","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"`multiply`, `prod` (`square` = 5)"},"1438":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-190","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"divide"},"1439":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-191","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**11**"},"1440":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-192","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"`divide` (`reciprocal` = 6)"},"1441":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-193","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"variance family"},"1442":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-194","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**2.5**"},"1443":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-195","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"`var`, `std` (square-and-sum: mostly adds, some multiplies)"},"1444":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-196","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"absolute / magnitude"},"1445":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-197","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**4**"},"1446":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-198","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"`abs` (`hypot` itself is complex-illegal: numpy raises)"},"1447":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-199","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"transcendental"},"1448":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-200","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**per op**"},"1449":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-201","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"`log` 2.25, `exp` 3.1, `sin`/`cos` 3.4, `tan` 3.6 — each from its complex closed form"},"1450":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-202","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"dense linalg"},"1451":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-203","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**4**"},"1452":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-204","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"`inv`, `solve`, `svd`, `det`, `qr`, `eig`, … (complex factorization ≈ 4× the real arithmetic)"},"1453":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-205","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"contraction (FMA)"},"1454":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-206","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**exact**"},"1455":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-207","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"`einsum`, `matmul`, `dot`, `inner`, `tensordot`, `vdot`, … — computed per call"},"1456":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-208","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"FFT"},"1457":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-209","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**1**"},"1458":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-210","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"no separate factor — the FFT formulas (`5N·log₂N`) already count the complex real-FLOPs: `fft.fft`, `fft.ifft`, `fft.rfft`"},"1459":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-211","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"complex-illegal"},"1460":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-212","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**raises**"},"1461":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-213","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"`floor`/`ceil`/`trunc`, bitwise & shift ops, `mod`/`fmod`/`floor_divide`, real-only math (`cbrt`, `i0`, `unwrap`, …) and the real-only random samplers — anything numpy rejects on complex input"},"1462":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-214","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"Composite `counted_custom` ops carry a per-op derived factor of the same kind (for example\n`polyval` 4, `cross` 4.7, `interp` 3, `cov`/`corrcoef` 4). Two subtleties are worth\nstating explicitly."},"1463":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-215","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**Contraction is billed exactly, not with a flat factor.** A length-`K` dot product is `K`\nmultiplies + `K − 1` adds (real `flop_cost = 2K − 1`). In complex arithmetic that is `K`\ncomplex multiplies + `K − 1` complex adds = `6K + 2(K − 1) = 8K − 2` real FLOPs. The ratio\n`(8K − 2) / (2K − 1)` is `6` at `K = 1`, `≈ 4.13` at `K = 8`, and approaches `4` as\n`K → ∞` — it is **not** a constant. So the contraction family carries the sentinel\n`\"exact\"`, and the engine computes the complex total per call directly from the same\naccumulation that produced `flop_cost`: `6·(multiplies) + 2·(adds)`, with the multiply and\nadd counts read straight off the einsum cost object. A single flat factor would invite\n**alias-shopping** — route a multiply-heavy computation through the contraction whose flat\nfactor is cheapest. Exact per-call billing removes the arbitrage: `einsum('i,i->i', z, z)`\n(a pure elementwise product, `K = 1`, all multiplies) bills factor 6, identical to\n`multiply(z, z)`, not a discounted 4."},"1464":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-216","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**FFT is priced in at factor 1.** The FFT `flop_cost` of `5N·log₂N` is *already* a count\nof real FLOPs, so its `complex_factor` is `1.0`; applying a second complex factor would\ndouble-charge. Derivation: a radix-2 FFT does `(N/2)·log₂N` butterflies; each butterfly is\none complex multiply (6 real FLOPs) + two complex adds (2 × 2 = 4 real FLOPs) = 10 real\nFLOPs; `10 × (N/2)·log₂N = 5N·log₂N`. The complex structure is baked into `flop_cost`, so\n`complex_factor` stays `1`."},"1465":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-217","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#complex-arithmetic-from-first-principles","content":"**Guaranteed coverage.** Every charged op carries an explicit `complex_factor` (a number,\n`\"exact\"`, or `\"illegal\"`); `tests/test_complex_factor_completeness.py` fails the build if\nany charged op is unclassified. An op with no classification is necessarily\nfree / movement / blacklisted and bills factor `2` — a complex value is two real\ncomponents, and relocating one still prices one unit per component even though no\narithmetic happens."},"1466":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-218","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"The billing dtype follows all work the call performs: the selected compute loop and any\ndestination buffer it materializes. Each op declares those participants at its deduct\nsite. Ordinary operands promote with `np.result_type`; Python scalars follow NumPy's\nNEP 50 weak promotion, so `f32_array * 2.0` stays float32 (the scalar does not up-promote\nthe array). Single-output binary pointwise ufuncs — and their `ufunc.outer` spellings —\nderive their compute price from NumPy's complete resolved loop signature, including every\narithmetic input slot and every output slot. That complete signature also determines\nwhether the registry's complex-arithmetic factor applies. The promoted operands provide an\ninput-rate floor: it can raise only `dtype_rate`, and does not invent complex arithmetic\nwhen NumPy resolves a non-complex numeric loop (for example, a predicate's boolean\noutput). `out=` then joins as a separate participant under the\nwidest-participating-buffer doctrine."},"1467":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-219","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"**A control operand is not an arithmetic operand.** One ufunc slot in NumPy's table names\na number that *steers* the arithmetic instead of being arithmetic, and it prices neither\nthe loop nor the floor. `ldexp(x1, x2)` computes `x1 * 2**x2`: `x2` is the exponent, and\nNumPy ships `ei->e` / `fi->f` / `di->d` (and their `l` variants) precisely so a wide\nexponent does not drag the mantissa up a precision tier. `ldexp(f32, int32_array)`\nresolves `fi->f`, computes `float32`, and bills at the float32 rate (`1000` for a\n1,000-element call) — identical to `ldexp(f32, int8_array)`, because it is identical\nmantissa work producing identical values. Membership is derived from the running NumPy's\nloop table rather than a hard-coded list (a slot qualifies iff NumPy has a loop producing\nan inexact result from an integral value there, and no loop accepting an inexact value\nthere); swept across NumPy's entire ufunc namespace that predicate selects exactly one\nslot, `ldexp`'s exponent. This is the input-side mirror of the price-neutral `frexp`\nexponent *output* described below, and of what `ufunc.at` has always billed for this\nfamily."},"1468":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-220","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"Worked consequences:"},"1469":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-221","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"**Mixed kinds promote up.** `matmul(int32_array, float32_array)` resolves to `float64`\n(numpy's promotion for that mix) and bills at the float64 rate — the same as a genuine\nfloat64 matmul. There is no discount for feeding narrow-looking operands."},"1470":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-222","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"**For these binary spellings, `dtype=` and `signature=` select computation, while\n`out=` prices materialization.** An explicit `dtype=` constrains NumPy's output DType;\nbilling still resolves every arithmetic input slot and output slot of the resulting\nloop. For example, `ldexp(int8, int64, dtype=float16)` runs the\n`float16,int64->float16` loop and therefore bills at the float16 rate — the forced\nmantissa width, not the exponent's. For symmetric loops,\n`multiply(f64, f64, dtype=float32)` casts both operands on read and runs the float32\nloop, so a 1000-element call bills at the float32 rate (`1000`), not at the operands'\nfloat64 rate. A forced `signature=` (or its `sig=` alias) likewise prices the exact\nloop NumPy executes, including a forced complex loop's registry complex factor; an\nexplicit `casting=` mode participates in that same resolution. `out=` is a separate\nparticipant: it cannot narrow compute, but a wider destination can raise the bill. Thus\n`multiply(f64, f64, out=float32_arr)` still computes and bills float64 (`2000`), while\n`multiply(f32, f32, out=float64_arr)` also bills `2000` because it materializes a\ngenuine float64 buffer. This keeps `out=` casting at `astype` parity: a wider `out=`\ncosts what the equivalent cast costs, and a narrower `out=` never discounts the loop."},"1471":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-223","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"**`dtype=` and `out=` compose for these binary spellings.**\n`add(int8, int8, dtype=float16, out=float64_arr)` computes with the float16 loop but\nbills at the float64 rate under the widest-participating-buffer doctrine; without\n`out=`, the same explicit loop bills at the float16 rate."},"1472":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-224","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"**For these binary spellings, a complex destination does not turn a non-complex loop\ninto complex arithmetic.** When a loop with no complex signature slot stores into a\ncomplex `out=`, the destination still participates in width billing, but computation\nkeeps its resolved classification. The call uses a store-only complex factor of `2.0`\ninstead of the ufunc's complex-arithmetic factor."},"1473":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-225","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"**A destination NumPy would have allocated anyway is price-neutral.** The rule above\nprices a destination as a buffer the call materializes — but two kinds of destination\nare not stores of the computed value at all, and naming them must cost exactly what\nletting NumPy allocate them costs. A **multi-output** op can have an output whose dtype\nbelongs to the op signature rather than to its arithmetic: `frexp`'s second output is\nalways `int32`, whatever precision the mantissa runs at, so `frexp(a_f32, out=(m_f32,\ne_i32))` bills what `frexp(a_f32)` bills (`20000` for a 10,000-element call — `frexp` is\n`nout=2`, so its own `flop_cost` is already `2 × numel(output)`, see\nElementwise), not the float64 rate that\n`result_type(float32, float32, int32)` would have promoted to. An\n**index reduction**'s destination holds positions, not values: `argmin`/`argmax`/\n`nanargmin`/`nanargmax` return `intp` regardless of the input's precision, so\n`argmin(a_f32, out=intp_arr)` bills what `argmin(a_f32)` bills (`9999`). Widening past\nNumPy's own choice still widens the rate in both families — a float64 mantissa or an\nint64 exponent on `frexp` bills `40000` (double the natural `20000`, from the rate, not\nfrom `nout` a second time). Index reductions additionally constrain `out=`\nby **kind**: NumPy accepts any integer or boolean buffer at any width and refuses every\nfloat and complex one, and that refusal is decided before the reduction runs, so it\ncosts `0`."},"1474":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-226","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"**A call NumPy has no loop for costs `0`.** Operand dtypes NumPy cannot run the\noperation on — `bitwise_and(f32, f32)`, `ldexp(f32, uint64)`, `invert(f32)`,\n`subtract(bool_, bool_)`, and every `reduce`/`accumulate`/`reduceat` of a ufunc with no\nsame-dtype loop, such as `ldexp` — are refused above the deduct site rather than billed\nand then raised. The question is settled with `ufunc.resolve_dtypes`, which is the same\nresolution NumPy's own dispatcher performs, so the exception a caller sees is\nbyte-identical in type and message to the one the unwrapped call raises. This is the\nsame reject-before-billing rule as the `out=` kind constraint above and `svd(k=)`\nbelow; before it applied to dtypes, `gcd.reduce` on a float array deducted 1584 FLOPs\nfor a call that never ran. Where flopscope has a refusal of its own for the same\noperands — a non-numeric dtype, or a complex operand to a complex-illegal op — that one\nstill takes precedence, so those keep raising `UnsupportedDtypeError` with flopscope's\ndiagnostic rather than NumPy's bare \"no loop matched\"."},"1475":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-227","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"**A non-numeric destination is refused, not priced.** A `str_`/`bytes_`/`datetime64`/\n`timedelta64`/structured-void `out=` — object-free included — describes where the\nresult is stored, not the loop that produced it, and its real per-element cost is not\nthe fixed unit a flat rate would have to assume. Rather than dropping it from the\nresolution and letting the call bill at the operands' own rate (which would launder a\nwide or width-mismatched destination down to whatever the arithmetic already costs),\nit is kept in the resolution precisely so it is refused —\n`add(i64_arr, i64_arr, out=m8_arr, casting='unsafe')` raises `UnsupportedDtypeError`\nrather than billing `10` or `20`. An `object`-carrying destination is refused the same\nway (see Dtype and precision)."},"1476":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-228","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"**Casting/converting bills like `copy`.** `astype` and `asarray` bill `numel(input)`\nat the heavier of source/destination rate — via `heavier_billing_dtype`, not\n`result_type`, which would over-promote a cross-kind cast such as `float32 → int32`\nto float64 — whenever they perform real work: narrowing, widening, a value-changing\ncast (to-bool, float→int, complex→real), a same-dtype `astype` with the default\n`copy=True`, or any `asarray(x, dtype=other)` that actually converts the buffer. The\none free case is the genuine no-op: `astype(x, dtype, copy=False)` when `dtype`\nalready matches `x`'s dtype returns the identical object, and a dtype-free or\ndtype-matching `asarray(x)` performs no conversion — both bill `0` (see\nViews and metadata for the full rule). `copyto`, the\nin-place write primitive, is priced one unit per element written (per selected\nelement under `where=`), same-dtype or not."},"1477":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-229","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"**`real` / `imag` are free.** Extracting a component of a complex value is a\nview / constant-fill, not arithmetic — `flop_cost = 0`."},"1478":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-230","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#which-dtype-prices-a-call","content":"**Dtype-neutral bookkeeping declares `dtypes=()`.** Ops that carry no value dtype at\nall (for example `einsum_path`) resolve to rate `1.0`, factor `1.0`, so the rate table\ncannot perturb them. Ops with a *fixed* output dtype but no array operand — window\nfunctions, `fft.fftfreq`, the random *distribution* samplers — are **not** neutral: they\ndeclare their output dtype, whatever it is (float64 for the continuous draws, int64 for\nthe discrete ones), so their real arithmetic bills at that dtype's rate like everything\nelse. This does **not** cover the data-movement random ops (`choice`, `shuffle`,\n`permutation`, `Generator.permuted`), which reorder caller-supplied values of any\nnumeric dtype and *are* billed neutrally — their selection work is width-independent.\nSee Random."},"1479":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-231","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction-accumulators","content":"A reduction is billed at the dtype numpy actually accumulates in. The accumulator\nresolves in numpy's own order: an explicit `dtype=` argument (positional or keyword) if\ngiven — billed exactly as requested, wider or narrower, because that request *is* the\naccumulator numpy runs; else `out`'s dtype if an output array is given — this widens the\naccumulator when `out` is wider than the input, but a narrower `out` only casts the\nfinal store, so the loop keeps the input's own width; else the family default — integer\nand boolean inputs widen to the platform default integer for `sum`/`prod`/`cumsum`/\n`cumprod` (and their `nan`-prefixed variants), and to `float64` for `mean`/`var`/`std`,\nnever billed below the input's own rate. Both `numpy.trace` and `linalg.trace` widen the\nsame way `sum` does — a batched diagonal sum is still a sum — and the generic\n`ufunc.reduce` / `accumulate` / `reduceat` paths resolve their accumulator through the\nidentical rule: `np.add.reduce` is the same machinery `sum` runs on, so\n`np.add.reduce(int32_arr, dtype=int32)` bills exactly like `sum(int32_arr, dtype=int32)`."},"1480":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-232","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction-accumulators","content":"`reduceat`'s base cost follows numpy's own per-segment semantics rather than a flat\nper-element charge: for segment `i`, `indices[i] < indices[i+1]` reduces the elements\nbetween them (a length-`L` segment costs `L-1` applications, the same `n-1` convention\n`reduce` uses), while a non-monotonic pair (`indices[i] >= indices[i+1]`) is a plain\nelement copy with no arithmetic — the segment costs 0. The final segment always runs to\nthe end of the axis. The exact call-level base formula is\n`max(lanes × applications_per_lane, lanes × produced_cells_per_lane, 1)`. The first two\nentries are the variable-work terms: actual ufunc applications across segments and cells\nproduced per lane. The cell term accounts for singleton/copy branches that emit without\ninvoking the ufunc; the final term retains a call-level minimum of one. The resulting\nbase cost is priced through the standard resolved accumulator dtype rate, complex\nfactor, and inherited base-ufunc weight. Ordinary long segments remain\narithmetic-dominated; a single whole-axis segment of 1000 elements bills 999\napplications. When the reduced axis has length one, the produced-cell term can make\n`reduceat(a, [0])` bill more than `reduce(a)`; this is intentional because `reduceat`\nretains its per-produced-cell floor even when the segment performs no arithmetic.\n`ufunc.outer` is not itself an accumulating reduction, but an explicit\n`dtype=` on it resolves through the same request-is-the-loop rule: the dtype names the\nloop numpy actually runs, not a discount, so\n`np.multiply.outer(int32_arr, int32_arr, dtype=float64)` bills the float64 rate over the\nfull outer-product grid — double the default int32-loop total."},"1481":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-233","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction-accumulators","content":"Worked examples (production rates, 1000-element input, `sum`):"},"1482":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-234","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction-accumulators","content":"`sum(int32_data)` — implicit int64 accumulator → 999 × 2.0 = **1998**."},"1483":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-235","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction-accumulators","content":"`sum(int32_data, dtype=int32)` — explicit 32-bit accumulation is real 32-bit work →\n999 × 1.0 = **999**."},"1484":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-236","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction-accumulators","content":"`sum(float32_data, None, float64)` — a positional wide accumulator bills exactly like\nthe keyword spelling `sum(float32_data, dtype=float64)` → 999 × 2.0 = **1998**."},"1485":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-237","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction-accumulators","content":"`sum(float64_data, dtype=float32)` — numpy genuinely accumulates in float32 (operands\ncast to float32 on read), so the bill follows that narrower loop →\n999 × 1.0 = **999**."},"1486":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-238","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction-accumulators","content":"`trace` follows the identical rule at its own, much smaller element count:\n`linalg.trace` on an `8×8` int32 matrix bills its 8-element diagonal sum at the int64\nrate (**16**); the same shape in float32 stays at float32 (**8** — floats never widen).\nThe explicit accumulator carries through the top-level wrapper too:\n`trace(int32_matrix, dtype=int32)` bills **8**, at int32 — the same rate as the input,\nbecause an explicit accumulator request is honored exactly."},"1487":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-239","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction-accumulators","content":"Two spellings resolve differently because they compute differently. Pointwise `dtype=`\ncomputes narrow: operands are cast to the requested dtype before the elementwise loop\nruns, so the bill is narrow too. Pointwise `out=` computes at the operand-promoted width\nand only casts the result on the way into `out`, so the bill stays wide. Reductions\nfollow the same split one level up: `dtype=` sets the accumulator in both directions —\nnarrower or wider, exactly as requested; `out=` widens the accumulator when `out` is\nwider than the input, while a narrower `out` only casts the final store, so it never\nbills below the input's own rate."},"1488":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-240","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"Outside the accumulator machinery above, several numpy kernels simply run their\narithmetic at a wider dtype than the input's own — the bill follows where the\ncomputation actually happens, not the input's label:"},"1489":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-241","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"family"},"1490":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-242","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"integer / bool inputs compute in"},"1491":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-243","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"float inputs"},"1492":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-244","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"unary float-only ufuncs (`exp`, `sin`, `sqrt`, …)"},"1493":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-245","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"the same-size float (`int8`/`uint8`/`bool` → `float16`; `int16`/`uint16` → `float32`; `int32`/`int64`/`uint32`/`uint64` → `float64`) — `angle` is the one exception, flooring `bool` alone at `float64` (see below)"},"1494":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-246","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"unchanged"},"1495":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-247","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"binary float-only ufuncs except division (`arctan2`, `hypot`, `logaddexp`, …)"},"1496":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-248","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"NumPy-selected float loop (`bool`/`int8`/`uint8` → `float16`; `int16`/`uint16` → `float32`; wider integers → `float64`)"},"1497":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-249","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"unchanged — this replaces the old blanket-float64 rule for narrow integers"},"1498":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-250","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`divide` / `true_divide`"},"1499":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-251","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"integer pairs resolve to `float64`"},"1500":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-252","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"unchanged"},"1501":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-253","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"predicate ufunc family (`signbit`, and the `logical_and(isinf(x), signbit(x))` composites `isneginf`/`isposinf`)"},"1502":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-254","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"the same-size float, exactly as the unary float-only row above (`bool`/`int8`/`uint8` → `float16`; `int16`/`uint16` → `float32`; wider integers → `float64`) — NumPy publishes no integer `signbit` loop, so an integer operand is promoted before the sign test runs"},"1503":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-255","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"unchanged"},"1504":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-256","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"tolerance checks (`isclose`, `allclose`, and the symmetry validators `is_symmetric`/`as_symmetric`, whose billed `k·(7n−1)` *is* an `allclose`)"},"1505":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-257","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`float64`, whatever the integer's own width — NumPy's `isclose` casts its reference operand with `result_type(y, 1.)` before comparing, unconditionally (an explicit `rtol=0, atol=0` promotes just the same)"},"1506":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-258","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"unchanged — `float16` and `float32` keep their own loop"},"1507":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-259","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`float_power`"},"1508":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-260","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"NumPy's selected loop has a `float64` real minimum"},"1509":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-261","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`float64` real minimum — unchanged in effect"},"1510":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-262","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"FFT family"},"1511":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-263","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`complex128` (no size-mapping — even `int8` runs the `complex128` path)"},"1512":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-264","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`float16`/`float32` → `complex64`; `float64` → `complex128`"},"1513":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-265","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"LAPACK-backed `linalg.*`"},"1514":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-266","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`float64`"},"1515":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-267","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"unchanged — single-precision drivers stay single"},"1516":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-268","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"mean-shaped composites (`average`, `median`, `gradient`, `percentile`, `nanmedian`, `nanpercentile`, …)"},"1517":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-269","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`float64`, regardless of the integer's own width (no size-mapping, unlike the unary-ufunc row above)"},"1518":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-270","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"unchanged"},"1519":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-271","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"always-float64 composites (`polyfit`, `polyint`, …)"},"1520":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-272","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`float64`"},"1521":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-273","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`float64` — forced even from `float32`"},"1522":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-274","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"The unary rows report the same thing. A ufunc-backed unary op is priced on the loop\nNumPy resolves for its operand, read from NumPy's own loop table at call time -- there\nis no per-op list of \"float-only\" names to keep in step with new NumPy releases. The\nfew unary entries flopscope still names are the COMPOSITES (`angle`, `i0`, `sinc`,\n`isneginf`, `isposinf`, and `fix` before NumPy 2.1), which publish no loop to resolve\nbecause they are Python functions built on other ufuncs."},"1523":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-275","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"The binary ufunc rows above report NumPy loop-table results, not operation-name floors\nimposed by flopscope. In particular, `float_power`'s narrowest real and complex\nsignatures are `dd->d` and `DD->D`, respectively, so `complex64` promotes to\n`complex128` — unchanged in effect."},"1524":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-276","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`exp` prices its size-mapped loop directly: on a 1000-element input, `int8` and `int16`\nboth still bill the `1.0` rate (**16000** — float16 and float32 share the baseline rate),\nwhile `int32`/`int64` bill the `2.0` float64 rate (**32000**)."},"1525":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-277","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`angle` follows that same size-mapped loop for every actual integer/unsigned width, but\n`bool` is a documented exception: on a 1000-element input, `angle(bool_data)` bills the\n`float64` rate (**32000**) while `angle(int8_data)` and `sin(bool_data)` both bill the\n`float16` rate (**16000**)."},"1526":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-278","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"The two predicate rows are the reason this table is keyed on what a kernel **computes\nin**, never on what it returns. Every op in them answers `bool`, so an output-derived\nrule would price them all at the baseline rate: on a 1000-element input,\n`signbit(int32_data)` bills the `float64` rate (**2000**), the same as\n`signbit(float64_data)`, while `signbit(int16_data)` bills the `float32` rate (**1000**)\n— and `isclose(int8_a, int8_b)` bills the `float64` rate (**12000**) because the\ntolerance core runs there, even though `isclose(float16_a, float16_b)` bills **6000**.\nThe weights are untouched; only the dtype rate follows the promotion."},"1527":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-279","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"For a worked binary example, `hypot(int8, int8)` selects a float16 loop and\n`hypot(int16, int16)` selects a float32 loop; both bill the baseline dtype rate `1.0`.\nBy contrast, `divide(int16, int16)` resolves to and bills float64 at rate `2.0`. Billing\ninspects the selected **complete** loop signature and then floors it by the promoted raw\ninput rate. That prevents a narrow output loop — or any other narrow slot — from\ndiscounting wider inputs; it does not regress to an output-slot-only rule."},"1528":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-280","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"`fft.fft` of a length-1024 (`N`) signal has a `5N·log₂N` = 51200 real-FLOP count before\nthe dtype rate; an int32 signal bills that count at the complex128 rate (**102400**),\nwhile a float32 signal keeps the complex64 rate and bills the raw **51200** unscaled."},"1529":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-281","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"A single non-inexact operand forces `linalg.*`'s promotion, regardless of how wide the\n*other* operand is: an `8×8` `linalg.solve(int32_matrix, int32_vector)` bills float64\n(**938**), and so does mixing kinds — `solve(bool_matrix, float32_vector)` on the same\nshape bills the identical **938**, exactly as if the matrix were genuinely float64. The\nall-float32 system keeps the single-precision driver and bills half that (**469**)."},"1530":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-282","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"Integer contractions keep their input's own rate: `matmul`, `dot`, `einsum`, and\n`matrix_power` with a non-negative exponent genuinely run integer arithmetic\n(`matmul(int32, int32)` stays int32). `matrix_power` with a *negative* exponent is the\nexception — numpy inverts the matrix first, which routes through the same LAPACK\nfloat64 promotion as `linalg.inv`: a `4×4` `matrix_power(int32_matrix, -1)` bills\nfloat64 (**224**) while `matrix_power(int32_matrix, 2)` stays int32 (**112**), and\n`matrix_power(float32_matrix, -1)` keeps the single-precision driver (**112**) even\nwhile inverting."},"1531":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-283","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"Not every widening composite forces float64 unconditionally, and not every one lands on\nfloat64 at all — the billed dtype is always whatever `resolved_dtype` records for that\nspecific call, not one fixed target per op family. `roots` builds a companion matrix and\nfinds its eigenvalues, so it inherits `linalg.eig`'s dtype rule directly: an integer\ninput computes float64, but a float32 input keeps its own single-precision driver.\n`poly` (rebuilding coefficients from roots) runs a different algorithm — iterative\nconvolution, not eigendecomposition — but resolves its dtype the same preserve-if-inexact\nway. Neither belongs in the always-float64 row above. `vander` promotes through\n`numpy.promote_types(x.dtype, int)`, the same kind of rule a reduction accumulator uses:\na narrow integer lands on the platform `int64` (`vander(int32_x)` bills its int64 rate,\nnot float64), while float and complex inputs still land on `float64` / `complex128`."},"1532":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-284","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"The generic `ufunc.outer` and `ufunc.at` methods apply this same per-element promotion\nto their base ufunc, so there is no method-shaped way around it:\n`np.hypot.outer(int32_arr, int32_arr)` bills the float64 rate over its full\nouter-product grid, and `np.exp.at(int32_arr, ...)` bills the float64 rate over the\nindices it touches — identical to calling `hypot` or `exp` directly."},"1533":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-285","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"Zero-work contractions charge **0** regardless of dtype. `einsum('ij->ji', z)` is a pure\ntranspose — no multiply-adds happen — and a `matmul` with an empty operand has no terms\nto sum, so both bill 0 even when `z` is complex128; a same-shape *non-empty* complex128\nmatmul still bills its exact contraction total (see Contraction is billed\nexactly)."},"1534":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-286","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"Zero *arithmetic* is not zero *work* once a destination is named. `einsum` is the one\ncontraction that does not forward `out=` to NumPy — NumPy writes its own buffer and the\nwrapper then copies that buffer into the caller's — so `einsum(subs, …, out=dest)` bills\nits contraction total **plus** a `copyto` of `numel(dest)`, at the same price\n`fnp.copyto(dest, result)` charges for the identical write. The step-2 rule applies to it\nlike any other materializing copy; without the charge, `einsum('ij->ji', z, out=dest)`\nwould be a full transpose of any size for nothing. The viewing form is unaffected: with no\ndestination NumPy returns a view, nothing is written, and 0 stays correct."},"1535":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-287","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"**Guaranteed coverage.** `tests/test_compute_dtype_conformance.py` probes every charged\nregistry op with a discriminating int32 input (or records why it is exempt) and asserts\nthe billed rate is at least the rate of both the NEP-50-promoted input dtype and numpy's\nactual result dtype — an op that quietly starts computing in a wider dtype than it bills\nfails the build instead of shipping an undercount. The sweep covers charged *registry*\nops; the dynamic ufunc-method surface (`.outer`, `.reduceat`, `.at`, `.reduce`,\n`.accumulate`, which bill under per-call op names like `hypot.outer` rather than registry\nkeys) is out of its reach and is locked by targeted tests in `tests/test_dtype_cost.py`\ninstead. There is a second, narrower carve-out inside the sweep: the index-output ops\n(`argmax`/`argmin`, `argsort`, `nonzero`/`flatnonzero`/`argwhere`, `searchsorted`,\n`count_nonzero`, `digitize`, `lexsort`, the `unique_*` tuple forms, and kin) are exempt\nfrom the **result**-dtype half of the assertion, because they return `int64` index arrays\nwhatever the operand's width — their result dtype says nothing about the arithmetic they\ndid. They are held to an **operand**-dtype floor instead, probed at `float64` so the\nassertion can actually be violated: the comparison work is done at the operand's width\nand must be billed there. A second pass narrows the same probes to `float32` and requires\nthe billed rate to follow the operand down, which is what separates billing the operand\nfrom billing the `int64` index result — those rate the same, so a floor alone cannot tell\nthem apart. `searchsorted` and `digitize` are held to over-resolving instead: their bin\nedges and needle promote the call, so they bill above the operand, which over-bills\nrather than under-bills. `tests/test_binary_ufunc_spelling_billing.py` pins\ncomplete-signature billing, narrow binary loops, the promoted-input floor,\ndirect/`outer` parity, explicit `dtype=`/`signature=` constraints plus wider-`out=`\ncomposition, descriptor-safe operand metadata, and store-only complex billing for the\nsingle-output binary paths."},"1536":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-288","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#ops-that-compute-wider-than-their-inputs","content":"Reproduce any of these yourself: run the call inside a `BudgetContext` and read\n`op_log[-1].resolved_dtype` alongside `flops_used`."},"1537":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-289","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"Billed FLOPs for an `N = 100` elementwise call under production rates (`multiply`: weight\n1, factor 6; `add`: weight 1, factor 2; `sin`: weight 16, factor 3.4):"},"1538":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-290","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"op"},"1539":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-291","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"float32"},"1540":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-292","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"float64"},"1541":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-293","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"complex64"},"1542":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-294","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"complex128"},"1543":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-295","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"`multiply`"},"1544":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-296","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"100"},"1545":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-297","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"200"},"1546":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-298","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"600"},"1547":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-299","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"1200"},"1548":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-300","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"`add`"},"1549":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-301","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"100"},"1550":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-302","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"200"},"1551":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-303","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"200"},"1552":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-304","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"400"},"1553":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-305","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"`sin`"},"1554":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-306","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"1600"},"1555":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-307","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"3200"},"1556":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-308","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"5440"},"1557":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-309","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"10880"},"1558":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-310","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"Reading one cell: `multiply` on `complex128` = `int(100 × 2.0 × 6.0 × 1.0) = 1200` —\n`flop_cost` 100, float64-component rate 2.0, complex-multiply factor 6.0, baseline weight\n1.0. `add` on `complex64` = `int(100 × 1.0 × 2.0 × 1.0) = 200`: a complex add's factor is\nonly 2, so it costs far less than the complex multiply. `sin` carries its weight-16\ntranscendental tier and a 3.4 complex factor, so `complex128` =\n`int(100 × 2.0 × 3.4 × 16) = 10880`."},"1559":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-311","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#worked-examples","content":"Contraction is exact rather than a flat factor: a `matmul` of two `(8, 8)` matrices bills\n`960` real (`2·8³ − 8²`), `1920` at float64, and `3968` at complex64 — the engine's exact\n`6·512 + 2·448` (512 scalar multiplies, 448 adds), a ratio of `≈ 4.13`, not `4`. At\ncomplex128 it is `7936` (that exact total × the 2.0 rate). These values are pinned in\n`tests/test_dtype_cost.py`."},"1560":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-312","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#on-packing","content":"**Metering does not establish competition eligibility.** For Phase 2 of the\nARC White-Box Estimation Challenge,\nthe official Rules are the source of truth and take precedence over this reference.\nA submission's score benefit must derive from its estimation method. The Sponsor\nmay invalidate, re-score, or disqualify submissions whose benefit instead derives\nfrom how computation is accounted."},"1561":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-313","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#on-packing","content":"**Do not pack independent values into one machine element to obtain an accounting\nadvantage.** The Rules include the example of packing booleans into a wider integer\nand processing them with bitwise operations. Metering every operation, including\npacking and unpacking, does not make such an accounting advantage permitted."},"1562":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-314","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#on-packing","content":"Choosing a lower-precision dtype or quantizing values is not, by itself, this\npacking technique; those choices still have to satisfy the Rules. The shared\n`1.0` rate for dtypes at or below 32 bits does not grant permission to use packed\ncomputation. Ask the competition organizers about uncertain techniques."},"1563":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-315","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#on-packing","content":"The following examples describe billing behavior, not permission:"},"1564":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-316","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#on-packing","content":"**Complex packing in this example loses.** Two real multiplies of 100-element\nfloat32 arrays bill `200`; folding the two payloads into one complex64 multiply bills `600` (factor 6) — a\n`3×` loss. At matmul scale the packed complex matmul bills `3968` against the `1920` of\nthe two honest real matmuls — roughly `2×` worse, and that is before any pack/unpack\noverhead."},"1565":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-317","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#on-packing","content":"**32-into-64 packing in this example breaks even at best.** Two float32 multiplies bill\n`2 × 100 = 200`; one float64 multiply carrying both payloads bills `100 × 2.0 = 200` — identical, and that is\n*before* the arithmetic to pack the lanes in and unpack the results out, which is itself\ncharged. This example is break-even-or-losing."},"1566":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-318","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#on-packing","content":"**Sub-32-bit lane tricks** (packing several int8 or int16 payloads into a wider lane) can\nproduce an accounting advantage, since everything at or below 32-bit shares the\n`1.0` rate. The meter does not automatically eliminate this advantage; the Phase 2\nfairness rule above applies even when the gain is small."},"1567":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-319","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#on-packing","content":"Billing follows the loop numpy actually runs, in both directions: an explicit narrow\n`dtype=` — pointwise, or a reduction's accumulator — bills narrow because the arithmetic\ngenuinely happens at that width, trading precision for a cheaper bill exactly as\nrequested. `out=` alone never narrows a loop, so it cannot be used to buy a narrow bill\nfor wide compute. These dtype-resolution rules explain the charge; they do not\nreplace the competition's fairness requirements."},"1568":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-320","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"The cost model includes safeguards against doing expensive work while being billed\ncheaply. These are accounting safeguards, not a guarantee that every possible\naccounting advantage is prevented or permitted; see On packing.\nThe two threats are **under-count** (an op billed below its\ntrue cost) and **substitution arbitrage** (routing the same work through a\ncheaper-billed but equivalent op). The model defends against both with invariants,\neach backed by a CI-enforced test you can open and read:"},"1569":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-321","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"Invariant"},"1570":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-322","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"What it guarantees"},"1571":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-323","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"Enforced by"},"1572":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-324","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**Faithful cost**"},"1573":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-325","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"each `flop_cost` is the real standard-algorithm op count, with every shape/algorithm constant inside `flop_cost`"},"1574":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-326","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"per-op evidence in §Cost by family; `test_cost_constant_unification.py`, `test_cost_formula_vs_code.py`"},"1575":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-327","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**Weight-tier policy**"},"1576":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-328","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"every active weight ∈ `{0, 1, 4, 16}`; arithmetic ops are 0 or 1; **no algorithm constant in a weight**"},"1577":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-329","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`test_weight_tier_policy.py`"},"1578":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-330","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**No substitution arbitrage**"},"1579":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-331","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"a bit-identical alias cannot bill cheaper than its canonical (e.g. `acos` *is* `arccos` — the 16× ufunc-alias fix); equivalent contractions (`dot`/`inner`/`matmul`/`einsum`) share one cost engine"},"1580":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-332","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`test_ufunc_alias_parity.py`, `test_random_weight_aliasing.py`; the shared einsum engine (§Contraction)"},"1581":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-333","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**No unpriceable or mispriceable dtype**"},"1582":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-334","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"a non-numeric dtype (`dtype.kind` outside the allowlist `\"biufc\"` — object, string, bytes, structured/void, datetime64, timedelta64) is refused before any charge — as an operand, an explicit `dtype=`, or an `out=` destination — because it either has unbounded per-element cost (object) or a real per-element cost no flat rate captures (the rest); a dtype that is still zero-itemsize once NumPy materialises it is the one exception, since it carries no data either way — `'U0'`/`'S0'` are not, because NumPy promotes them to `'U1'`/`'S1'` on allocation"},"1583":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-335","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`tests/test_object_dtype_ban.py`"},"1584":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-336","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**No cheap in-op path**"},"1585":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-337","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"top-k `svd(k=)` cannot yield a *full* decomposition below full price (the `min(4mnk, economy)` cap + `k ≥ min → full` guard); invalid `k` (`< 1` or `> min(m, n)`) is rejected before any billing"},"1586":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-338","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`test_svd_topk_cost.py` (cap / guard / monotonicity); `test_linalg.py` (invalid-`k` `ValueError`)"},"1587":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-339","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**A symmetry tag matches the buffer it is attached to**"},"1588":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-340","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"symmetry validation uses a tolerance (`np.allclose`, `atol=1e-6`, `rtol=1e-5`) while the cost model reads the tag as exact — it prices every position in an orbit after the first as a redundant degree of freedom and does not read the buffer again. The two untrusted ingress points, `as_symmetric(data, symmetry=…)` and a *bare, top-level* `SymmetricTensor(data, symmetry=…)`, therefore validate the claim (raising `SymmetryError` on a mismatch, charging `k·(7n − 1)` for a genuine one, `k` = non-identity generators, `n = data.size`) and then canonicalize: each orbit takes the value at its lexicographically smallest index, so values that survived only within the tolerance do not reach the tag. Data already exactly invariant is passed through unchanged, so the charge and the zero-copy view semantics are unaffected; the caller's array is never modified. Symmetry a flopscope op derives *internally* (e.g. `exp` propagating an already-validated operand's tag, or a slice/transpose view) is exempt, since it never carries a fresh caller-supplied claim. &#x2A;*Scope:** this is a property of those two ingress points, not a package-wide invariant — a Reynolds projection sums each orbit in a fixed element order, so its own output is typically invariant only to about an ulp, as is a symmetric matmul. That residue is rounding rather than caller-placed information, so it is sound for accounting; code needing bit-exactness should use an ingress point or `_canonical_symmetry.is_exactly_invariant`. In-process code can attach a tag regardless (`arr.view(SymmetricTensor)` plus an attribute assignment needs nothing from this package), so the boundary that holds is the wire: the server dispatches registered ops only, and none of those names is registered"},"1589":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-341","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`tests/test_symmetry_canonicalization.py`; `tests/test_symmetric_tensor_new_validation.py`; `tests/test_symmetric_cost.py` (`k·(7n−1)` rate)"},"1590":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-342","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**Free-tier discipline**"},"1591":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-343","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"weight 0 is limited to views/metadata, untouched (zero-page or uninitialized) allocation, and the narrow `astype`/`asarray` no-op (`copy=False` with an already-matching dtype; a dtype-free or dtype-matching `asarray`) — every other cast or copy, including a same-dtype `astype(copy=True)`, bills `numel` like `copy`. Any **metered** op that writes a new buffer — copied, replicated, constant-filled, gathered, or scattered — carries weight ≥ 1 (ndarray methods inherited from numpy are outside the meter by design — see §The meter boundary). Every value-test is charged wherever it hides: `a.nonzero()` (method), `where(1-arg)`, `argwhere`, `flatnonzero`, `count_nonzero`"},"1592":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-344","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`test_weight_tier_policy.py`; `test_data_movement_free_tier.py` (free-labels consistency guard)"},"1593":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-345","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**No free-gather discount**"},"1594":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-346","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"a computed-index gather (`take`, `take_along_axis`, `choose`) is metered at the access tier (weight 4.0) like any other non-sequential read, so precomputing a look-up table and then gathering from it no longer buys a categorical discount; only genuine view-indexing (a static/basic index, `arr[i]`) stays free"},"1595":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-347","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`test_data_movement_free_tier.py`; §Copy and gather"},"1596":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-348","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**Complex packing example non-profitable**"},"1597":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-349","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"folding two real payloads into one complex op's real/imag lanes bills the op's true complex structure (`multiply` factor 6, matmul exact `≈4.13×`), so the pack costs more than the honest real work it replaces"},"1598":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-350","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`tests/test_dtype_cost.py` (packing tests)"},"1599":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-351","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**32-into-64 packing example break-even-or-losing**"},"1600":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-352","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"a 64-bit op bills `2×` a 32-bit op (`dtype_rate`), so packing two 32-bit payloads into one 64-bit lane is break-even before pack/unpack overhead; billing follows the loop numpy actually runs, so an explicit narrow `dtype=` only bills narrow when the compute is genuinely that narrow, and `out=` alone never shrinks the loop"},"1601":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-353","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`tests/test_dtype_cost.py` (width-packing tests); `tests/test_unary_out_never_narrows.py` (the unary float-loop family's whole `out=` grid)"},"1602":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-354","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**End-to-end billing**"},"1603":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-355","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"production billing is pinned per weight tier `{0,1,4,16}` (catches a silent weight regression); the retired `8.0` tier is documented as retired rather than silently dropped"},"1604":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-356","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`test_production_weight_billing.py`"},"1605":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-357","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"**No free path-search wall time**"},"1606":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-358","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`opt_einsum.contract_path` runs as pure Python inside the counted wrapper, never through `_call_user_code`, so its wall time books to the unbilled `flopscope_overhead_time_s` bucket rather than to backend FLOPs or residual. Once operand count `k ≥ _LARGE_K_THRESHOLD` (8), `optimize=` is resolved through an **allowlist**, not a denylist: only strategies known to run in linear time (`'greedy'`, `'eager'`, `'opportunistic'`, `'random-greedy'`, `'random-greedy-128'`), `False` (no search), and an explicit user-supplied path (a `list`/`tuple` — the order is already decided, so there is no search to bound) pass through verbatim; everything else — every current `opt_einsum` alias for an exhaustive or superlinear search (`'auto'`, `'auto-hq'`, `'optimal'`, `'branch-all'`, `'branch-2'`, `'branch-1'`, `'dp'`, `'dynamic-programming'`), `True`, `None`, a custom `PathOptimizer`, and any future `opt_einsum` string this list doesn't recognize — downgrades to `'greedy'`. The allowlist replaced an earlier denylist that missed the `'dynamic-programming'` alias (the exact same function object as `'dp'`, registered under a second name) plus `'auto-hq'`/`'branch-1'`; a live repro parked 45.75s of free wall time via `optimize='dynamic-programming'` at k=20 before the allowlist closed it"},"1607":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-359","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"`_resolve_optimize_for_k` in `_einsum.py`; `tests/test_einsum_path_search_billing.py`, `tests/accumulation/test_path_aware_cost.py::test_large_k_auto_fallback_to_greedy` and `::test_large_k_covers_every_opt_einsum_path_alias` (the latter iterates `opt_einsum.paths._PATH_OPTIONS` directly, so a future `opt_einsum` release adding a new exhaustive-search alias fails CI instead of silently reopening the hole)"},"1608":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-360","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#non-exploitability","content":"An auditor can read this table top-to-bottom and, for each claim, open the named test\nto see exactly what guarantees it. The first two rows are the load-bearing ones: an exact\n`flop_cost` defeats under-count, and the weight-tier policy (no constant in a weight)\ndefeats the family of arbitrage exploits where a high-constant op is re-tiered cheaply."},"1609":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-361","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"Every invariant above scopes to **metered** ops — the `fnp.*` surface that routes\nthrough flopscope. `FlopscopeArray` is an `np.ndarray` subclass that intercepts the\nufunc/operator protocol, so arithmetic is counted; methods it inherits unchanged from\nnumpy are not. `.tobytes()`, `.tolist()`, `.data` and `__array__` therefore write a new\nbuffer for 0 billed FLOPs, and `fnp.frombuffer(a.tobytes())` reproduces `fnp.copy(a)`\nwithout the `numel` charge."},"1610":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-362","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"This is deliberate, and it is contained by **residual billing rather than by weight**.\nWhestbench scores effective compute as `C = F + λ·R`, where `R` is participant wall time\noutside metered dispatch and `λ = 1e11` FLOP/s. Anything materialised in participant\nPython is paid for in `R`, and λ sits far above the rate at which data movement converts\nwall time into saved FLOPs:"},"1611":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-363","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"achievable memory bandwidth"},"1612":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-364","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"billed FLOP/s saved"},"1613":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-365","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"residual FLOP/s charged"},"1614":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-366","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"net"},"1615":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-367","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"1 GB/s"},"1616":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-368","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"2.50e8"},"1617":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-369","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"1.00e11"},"1618":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-370","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"400× loss"},"1619":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-371","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"5 GB/s"},"1620":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-372","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"1.25e9"},"1621":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-373","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"1.00e11"},"1622":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-374","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"80× loss"},"1623":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-375","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"10 GB/s"},"1624":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-376","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"2.50e9"},"1625":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-377","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"1.00e11"},"1626":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-378","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"40× loss"},"1627":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-379","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"20 GB/s"},"1628":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-380","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"5.00e9"},"1629":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-381","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"1.00e11"},"1630":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-382","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"20× loss"},"1631":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-383","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"So the free-copy channel is real but strictly losing: at 1 billed FLOP per float32\nelement, substituting `fnp.frombuffer(a.tobytes() + b.tobytes())` for\n`fnp.concatenate([a, b])` saves 2e6 FLOPs and costs \\~1.8e8 residual FLOP-equivalents —\nabout 92× worse. Overlapping the copy with an in-flight metered op does not rescue it:\n`tobytes` holds the GIL, and breaking even requires hiding \\~99% of the copy."},"1632":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-384","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"Metering these methods would also be actively harmful, not merely conservative.\n`tobytes()` is on flopscope's own wire path (`_request_handler.py` serialises arrays with\n`arr.tobytes()`), so billing it would make the transport bill itself on every array\ntransfer, and `.tolist()` is the documented way to read outputs back at the end of\n`predict()`. The boundary is drawn where it is so that getting data *out* is never a\ncharged operation."},"1633":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-385","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"The place the cost model is genuinely sensitive is the narrower one where work happens\n*inside* the meter for a mispriced amount — which is what every invariant in the table\nabove is defending."},"1634":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-386","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"**Timing attribution inside a metered call** has three buckets, not two:\n`wall_time_s == flopscope_backend_time_s + flopscope_overhead_time_s +\nresidual_wall_time_s`, and `residual_wall_time_s` is defined as `wall − backend −\noverhead` — so `flopscope_overhead_time_s` (flopscope's own dispatch code: wrapper\npreambles, bookkeeping, path search) sits outside *both* billed FLOPs and the\nλ-rated residual `R`. It is meant to be small and bounded (dispatch bookkeeping),\nnot a channel a caller can inflate; see the **No free path-search wall time**\ninvariant above for the one case where an unbounded pure-Python computation\n(`opt_einsum.contract_path`) used to run inside it."},"1635":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-387","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"**NEP 18 dispatch coverage.** A second, narrower boundary gap sits in-process only (the\ngraded sandbox has no local `numpy` for a participant to import and mix in). `np.<func>(a,\nb)` where `a` is a `FlopscopeArray` and `b` a plain `ndarray` falls through to NumPy's own\n`__array_function__` protocol whenever `func` is not in\n`FlopscopeArray._ARRAY_FUNCTION_DISPATCH`, and NumPy runs its default implementation\ndirectly on the raw buffers, so that work is not counted. The same call with every argument\na `FlopscopeArray` fails **closed** instead, with a `TypeError` (\"no implementation found\"). The following are\nexplicitly bound so a mixed call routes through the metered implementation the same as an\nall-`FlopscopeArray` call already does:"},"1636":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-388","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"`np.<func>`"},"1637":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-389","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"routes to"},"1638":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-390","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"`copyto`"},"1639":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-391","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"`fnp.copyto`"},"1640":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-392","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"`putmask`"},"1641":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-393","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"`fnp.putmask`"},"1642":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-394","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"`place`"},"1643":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-395","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"`fnp.place`"},"1644":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-396","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"`select`"},"1645":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-397","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"`fnp.select`"},"1646":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-398","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"`cov`"},"1647":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-399","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"`fnp.cov`"},"1648":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-400","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"`corrcoef`"},"1649":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-401","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"`fnp.corrcoef`"},"1650":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-402","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"`linalg.tensorsolve`"},"1651":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-403","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"`fnp.linalg.tensorsolve`"},"1652":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-404","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#the-meter-boundary","content":"`tests/test_array_function_dispatch_coverage.py` pins this floor — it forces the lazy\ndispatch-map build via `FlopscopeArray._get_array_function_dispatch()` and asserts each of\nthe seven above is present. Coverage is a floor, not a completeness guarantee; the bound set is the one the test pins."},"1653":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-405","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#cost-by-family","content":"Each family below is one **rule** + its **evidence/citation** + **representative ops**.\nThe rule is the part to audit; the per-op tables are kept where each op carries a\n*distinct* cited constant (linalg, FFT, polynomial, stats, window, random) because\nthose constants are the evidence — and because `ops.json`'s generated `cost_formula`\nis coarse for many composite ops (it records `per-operation` where the real formula is\nshape-dependent). For families whose members all share one rule (copy/gather, views),\nonly representatives are listed and the full set is a filter in\n`ops.json`."},"1654":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-406","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#elementwise-pointwise-unary-and-binary","content":"**Family rule**: `flop_cost = numel(output)` for a single-output op. A **multi-output\nufunc** (`divmod`, `frexp`, `modf` — the only NumPy ufuncs with `nout > 1` flopscope\ncounts) is priced as `nout` independent applications of the reference algorithm, so its\n`flop_cost = nout × numel(output)`: `divmod` bills what running `floor_divide` and `mod`\nseparately would bill, combined; `modf`/`frexp` bill twice a one-output unary of the same\nshape. The justification is **not** the write-metering principle above — arithmetic\nweight, not buffer traffic, dominates these ops, so that clause does not govern them.\nThe justification is the model's standing rule that pricing tracks a **standardized\nreference algorithm**, not whatever compute-sharing a specific backend implementation\nhappens to do — stated earlier as \"we bill the textbook standard-algorithm cost, not\nliteral BLAS/LAPACK\" (see Billing model & design\nprinciples) and already the basis for the FMA=2\nconvention and the `trapz`/`nanstd`/`linspace` fixes. A backend that computes several\noutputs more cheaply than the sum of independent calls does not earn its caller a\ndiscount; without this rule, an efficient combined op becomes a way to obtain two\ndeliverables for the price of one. Measured backend timing makes this explicit rather\nthan contradicting it: at N=500,000, `np.divmod` runs in \\~0.98x a single\n`np.floor_divide` (\\~0.49x `floor_divide`+`mod` combined) — NumPy's `divmod` genuinely\nshares the division and derives the remainder almost for free — yet flopscope prices it\nat `2×` `floor_divide` anyway, by the reference-algorithm rule above; the bill is a\ndeliberate ceiling on what a participant could otherwise obtain via an efficient\ncombined op, not a measurement of `divmod`'s own backend cost. `out=` does not multiply\nthis a second time — the `nout` scaling lives in `flop_cost` (the cell-count axis),\nwhich is independent of the `out=` destination-dtype-rate axis described in Which\ndtype prices a call; supplying `out=` widens the rate\nexactly as it would for any other op, never the cell count."},"1655":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-407","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#elementwise-pointwise-unary-and-binary","content":"**Baseline tier (weight 1.0)**: arithmetic (+, −, ×, ÷, √), rounding\n(ceil, floor, trunc, rint, around/round), sign/abs, logical (not, and, or,\nxor), bitwise (and, or, xor, invert, left\\_shift, right\\_shift), comparisons\n(equal, not\\_equal, greater, less, greater\\_equal, less\\_equal), copies\n(positive, negative, conj/conjugate, fabs, spacing,\nnan\\_to\\_num, isclose, isneginf, isposinf, deg2rad/degrees, rad2deg/radians,\nldexp, nextafter, copysign, heaviside, signbit), and their NumPy aliases. `modf` and\n`frexp` are also weight 1.0, but — being `nout=2` — bill `2 × numel(output)`, not\n`numel(output)`; see the family rule above.\n(`real`/`imag` are **not** here — component extraction is free; see\nView / free.)"},"1656":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-408","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#elementwise-pointwise-unary-and-binary","content":"**Transcendental tier (weight 16.0)**: exp, exp2, expm1, log, log2, log10,\nlog1p, cbrt, sin, cos, tan, sinh, cosh, tanh, arcsin, arccos, arctan,\narcsinh, arccosh, arctanh, sinc, i0, power, angle, and their NumPy 2.x\naliases (asin, acos, atan, asinh, acosh, atanh, atan2, pow)."},"1657":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-409","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#elementwise-pointwise-unary-and-binary","content":"**Moderate binary tier (weight 16.0)**: arctan2/atan2, hypot, logaddexp,\nlogaddexp2, floor\\_divide, mod/remainder, fmod, float\\_power. NumPy's selected\n`float_power` loop has no real signature narrower than `dd->d` and no complex signature\nnarrower than `DD->D`, so float32 operands bill as float64 and complex64 operands bill\nas complex128. This comes from NumPy's loop table, not a hand-maintained family mapping.\n`divmod` sits in this tier too, at `floor_divide`'s weight (see NumPy 2.x ufunc\naliases) — but being `nout=2`, its `flop_cost` is `2 ×\nnumel(output)`, so a bare `divmod` call bills exactly what running `floor_divide` and\n`mod` separately would bill, combined."},"1658":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-410","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#elementwise-pointwise-unary-and-binary","content":"**Iterative integer tier (weight 16.0)**: `gcd`, `lcm`. These sit at the weight-16 tier\nwithout being transcendental functions, and the reason is the same one the tier exists\nfor: the per-element kernel is a bounded multi-step loop, not a single instruction.\nNumPy computes `gcd` by the Euclidean algorithm — a remainder loop whose trip count\ngrows with the operand magnitude — and `lcm` runs that same loop and then a multiply and\na divide. Pricing them at the arithmetic tier would sell a genuinely iterative\nper-element computation at the price of an `add`. Both are integer-only\n(`complex_factor: illegal` — NumPy raises `TypeError` on complex input), so the complex\ntable below never applies to them; an `int64` operand pair bills\n`numel × 16 × 2` (weight 16.0 at the int64 rate 2.0)."},"1659":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-411","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#elementwise-pointwise-unary-and-binary","content":"**Basis**: DECLARED per-element FMA=2 convention and empirical calibration."},"1660":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-412","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#elementwise-pointwise-unary-and-binary","content":"**Complex dtypes**: each op bills its per-op `complex_factor` from the class table in\nDtype and precision — `add`/comparisons 2,\n`multiply` 6, `divide` 11, `abs` 4, transcendentals per op (`sin` 3.4, `log` 2.25); `conj`\nand `angle` price the same component floor (factor 2); rounding, bitwise, and\n`mod`-family ops are complex-illegal and raise."},"1661":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-413","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#elementwise-pointwise-unary-and-binary","content":"Source: `src/flopscope/_pointwise.py`."},"1662":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-414","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"**Family rule**: `flop_cost = numel(input) − numel(output)` (orbit-mapping\nmodel; one add or compare per element consumed by the reduction)."},"1663":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-415","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"Ops that do more than one accumulation pass carry the extra passes in\n`flop_cost` (never in the weight column): the variance family makes four\npasses (mean-sum, centre, square, variance-sum), `ptp` makes two (max + min)\nplus the per-output subtract, and `mean`/`average` add the per-output divide.\nA `nan*` variant adds a further `numel(input)` on top of its plain sibling's\nformula, for the `isnan` test pass the `nan*` form runs before reducing that\nits plain sibling does not — **but only where NumPy actually runs that pass**.\nNine of the factory-built `nan*` reductions go through NumPy's\n`_replace_nan`, which returns no mask for a non-inexact dtype, so an\n**integer or bool input runs no `isnan` pass and bills exactly like its plain\nsibling**; only float/complex input carries the surcharge. `nanmedian`,\n`nanpercentile`, and `nanquantile` instead go through `_remove_nan_1d`, which\ncalls `np.isnan&#x60; whatever the dtype, so those three carry it for every dtype.\n**`nanmax` and `nanmin` carry it at no dtype at all**: for any plain\nnon-object ndarray — which is every operand flopscope hands them — NumPy\ntakes a fast path that reduces with `fmax`/`fmin` and then tests the *reduced\noutput*, never the input, so there is no input-sized pass to charge and both\nbill exactly like `max`/`min`. Like every other\npass in these ops, the surcharge is **orbit-mapped**: on a symmetric operand it\ncounts unique elements, not dense `numel`. The pass is folded into the same\n`flop_cost` as the rest of the reduction, so it is charged as one additional\npass over the input priced at the op's own rate/factor — not a separate flat\nrate: it inherits the host op's complex factor (e.g. 6.0 on `nanprod`) and\nits resolved accumulator dtype (e.g. the widened int64 rate on an int32\ninput), the same way the variance family and `ptp` already blend multiple\npasses under one op-level factor."},"1664":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-416","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"Op"},"1665":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-417","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"flop\\_cost"},"1666":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-418","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"weight"},"1667":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-419","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"basis"},"1668":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-420","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"`sum`, `prod`, `max`, `min`, `any`, `all`, `nansum`, `nanmax`, `nanmin`, `nanprod`"},"1669":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-421","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"numel(input) − numel(output) (+numel(input) for `nansum`/`nanprod` on float/complex input only; `nanmax`/`nanmin` add nothing at any dtype — see the family rule)"},"1670":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-422","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"1.0"},"1671":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-423","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"DECLARED reduction skeleton (one add or compare per consumed element)"},"1672":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-424","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"`cumsum`, `cumprod`, `nancumsum`, `nancumprod`, `cumulative_sum`, `cumulative_prod`"},"1673":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-425","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"numel(input) − num\\_output\\_slices (= n−1 for a full 1-D scan; product of non-reduced dims otherwise) (+numel(input) for the `nan*` forms on float/complex input only)"},"1674":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-426","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"1.0"},"1675":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-427","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"DECLARED: scan accumulation; output shape = input shape so the generic `numel(in)−numel(out)` formula evaluates to 0 — these use the correct per-slice count instead"},"1676":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-428","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"`mean`, `average` (unweighted)"},"1677":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-429","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"numel(input)"},"1678":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-430","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"1.0"},"1679":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-431","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"DERIVED: reduction (numel−M) + M divides"},"1680":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-432","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-countin