g-model#reduction","content":"DERIVED: reduction (numel−M) + M divides, + the `nan*` isnan pass where NumPy runs it"},"1712":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-464","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"**Complex dtypes**: sum-type reductions (`sum`, `mean`, `cumsum`, `nansum`, …) bill factor\n2 (complex add); product reductions (`prod`, `cumprod`, `nanprod`) factor 6 (complex\nmultiply); the variance family (`var`, `std`, `nanvar`, `nanstd`) factor 2.5."},"1713":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-465","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#reduction","content":"Source: `src/flopscope/_pointwise.py`; reduction accumulation model in\n`src/flopscope/_accumulation/`."},"1714":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-466","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"Every op in this family is billed by **one shared, symmetry-aware engine**\n(`_resolve_cost_and_output_symmetry` → `einsum_cost`); the closed forms below are\nthat engine's output specialised to each op's shapes, not separately maintained\nconstants."},"1715":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-467","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"**Family rule:**"},"1716":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-468","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`(2K − 1)` is one length-`K` dot product: `K` multiplies + `K − 1` adds (FMA=2)."},"1717":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-469","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`K` = product of the **contracted** (summed) axis dimensions."},"1718":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-470","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`M` = number of output cells the engine computes. This is `prod(output dims)`\nfor a generic contraction, but the engine **reduces it to the unique-orbit\ncount when it can prove the output is symmetric** — when operands alias the\nsame array (`outer(v, v)`, `inner(A, A)`) or carry an `as_symmetric` tag. It\nnever invents savings: `A @ A` for a general `A` still costs the full\n`2n³ − n²`, because `A @ A` is not symmetric."},"1719":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-471","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"Op"},"1720":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-472","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"Contraction (`k` = contracted dim)"},"1721":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-473","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"flop\\_cost `= (2K − 1) × M`"},"1722":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-474","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`matmul`, `linalg.matmul`"},"1723":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-475","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model