","content":"`2N − 1`"},"1740":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-492","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`matvec`, `vecmat`"},"1741":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-493","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"matrix·vector / vector·matrix, contracting `k` → length-`m`"},"1742":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-494","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`m(2k − 1)`"},"1743":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-495","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`kron`"},"1744":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-496","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`(a,) ⊗ (b,)` of flattened operands — nothing summed, `K = 1`"},"1745":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-497","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`a.size × b.size`"},"1746":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-498","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`einsum`"},"1747":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-499","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"any subscripts"},"1748":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-500","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"whole-expression accumulation (below)"},"1749":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-501","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"**Symmetry savings** make `M` drop below `prod(output)` (here `v` is length `n`,\n`A` is `n × n`):"},"1750":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-502","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"Expression"},"1751":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-503","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"generic `M`"},"1752":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-504","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"symmetric `M`"},"1753":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-505","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"flop\\_cost"},"1754":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-506","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`outer(v, v)`"},"1755":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-507","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`n²`"},"1756":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-508","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`n(n+1)/2`"},"1757":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-509","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`n(n+1)/2`"},"1758":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-510","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`inner(A, A)`"},"1759":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-511","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`n²`"},"1760":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-512","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`n(n+1)/2`"},"1761":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-513","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`(2n − 1) · n(n+1)/2`"},"1762":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-514","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`einsum` runs the accumulation directly as `(K − 1)·M + α`, where `α` is the\nnumber of unique (output + contracted) index combinations — equal to `K·M` for a\nsingle clean contraction, but more general for multi-index or broadcast\nsubscripts. A multi-operand einsum (`≥ 3` operands) walks the `opt_einsum`\noptimal binary path and sums per-step costs. Batched/stacked variants of any row\nabove multiply the closed form by the batch size. The path search that picks\nthis binary order runs unbilled (see the **No free path-search wall time**\ninvariant in §Non-exploitability), so at `k ≥ 8`\noperands only an allowlisted, linear-time `optimize=` choice runs verbatim —\nevery other choice, including every `opt_einsum` alias for an exhaustive or\nsuperlinear search, is downgraded to `'greedy'` before the search runs."},"1763":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-515","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"**Compound linalg** ops are *chains* of matmuls, billed as the sum of their steps\nthrough the `matmul_cost(m, k, n)` helper — which itself delegates to\n`einsum_cost('ij,jk->ik', …)`, so each step equals a 2-D matmul by construction\n(no duplicated `2mkn − mn` constant to drift). `linalg.pinv` and `linalg.lstsq`\nbuild on the same helper."},"1764":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-516","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"Op"},"1765":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-517","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"flop\\_cost"},"1766":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-518","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"basis"},"1767":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-519","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`linalg.matrix_power`"},"1768":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-520","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`(⌊log₂ k⌋ + popcount(k) − 1) × matmul_cost(n, n, n)`"},"1769":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-521","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"repeated squaring"},"1770":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-522","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"`linalg.multi_dot`"},"1771":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-523","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"sum of optimal-chain matmul costs, each step `2mkn − mn`; the two-array form with a 0-d operand instead bills `numel(other)`, the scalar multiply NumPy performs there"},"1772":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-524","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"optimal chain order"},"1773":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-525","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"**Complex dtypes**: the contraction family is billed **exactly**, not with a flat factor.\nThe engine expands each call's `flop_cost` into `6·(multiplies) + 2·(adds)` from the same\naccumulation decomposition (see\nthe exact-contraction rule). For a length-`K`\ndot product the complex/real ratio is `(8K − 2)/(2K − 1)` (6 at `K=1`, `≈4.13` at `K=8`),\nso `einsum('i,i->i', z, z)` bills exactly like a complex `multiply`."},"1774":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-526","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#contraction-einsum-family","content":"All contraction ops use **weight 1.0** — the shape formulas already carry the\nfull FMA=2 cost. Source: `_pointwise.py` (op wrappers), `_einsum.py`\n(`_resolve_cost_and_output_symmetry`), `_flops.py` (`einsum_cost`,\n`matmul_cost`), `_accumulation/` (accumulation model)."},"1775":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-527","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#generator-linspace-arange-and-kin","content":"Op"},"1776":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-528","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#generator-linspace-arange-and-kin","content":"flop\\_cost"},"1777":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-529","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#generator-linspace-arange-and-kin","content":"basis"},"1778":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-530","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#generator-linspace-arange-and-kin","content":"source"},"1779":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-531","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#generator-linspace-arange-and-kin","content":"`arange`"},"1780":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-532","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#generator-linspace-arange-and-kin","content":"`2 × numel(output)`"},"1781":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-533","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model