rl":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#linalg-iterative-eigen--svd","content":"`10n³`, `n` = stripped companion dimension (leading and trailing zero coefficients removed before companion matrix is built)"},"1991":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-743","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#linalg-iterative-eigen--svd","content":"DERIVED: companion-matrix eigvals (delegates to eigvals\\_cost on trimmed degree)"},"1992":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-744","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#linalg-iterative-eigen--svd","content":"`_polynomial.py`; consistent with polynomial-table `roots` row"},"1993":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-745","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#top-k-truncated-svd","content":"`linalg.svd(..., k=)` and `linalg.svdvals(..., k=)` accept a top-k parameter.\nFor `1 ≤ k < min(m, n)` the billed cost is"},"1994":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-746","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#top-k-truncated-svd","content":"min(4·m·n·k, economy)"},"1995":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-747","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#top-k-truncated-svd","content":"where `economy` is the full thin/values-only cost above. `4·m·n·k` is the\nleading-order cost (FMA=2, Θ(mnk)) of a rank-k truncated SVD (two\nunavoidable passes over A). It is billed as the\n**standard truncated-algorithm cost of the operation** — consistent with how\nthis model bills direct-linalg ops at their textbook standard-algorithm count\nrather than literal BLAS/LAPACK work — even though the reference implementation\ncomputes the full economy SVD\nand slices (results stay exact). Unlike the full case, **values-only is not\nleading-order cheaper** for top-k. `k = min(m, n)` (all components) bills the\nfull economy cost, and the `full_matrices` full-U premium applies only to the\nfull decomposition (`k is None`); so a complete decomposition can never be\nobtained below full price. Invalid `k` (`< 1` or `> min(m, n)`) raises\n`ValueError`."},"1996":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-748","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#top-k-truncated-svd","content":"**Accepted residual:** because `4mnk < 6ab²+20b³` for all `k ≤ min(m, n)`, the\ntruncated rate applies up to `k = min(m, n) − 1`, so a caller can obtain up to\n`min(m, n) − 1` exact singular vectors at the truncated rate. The guard ensures\nthey can never obtain **all** `min(m, n)` components below full price."},"1997":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-749","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#top-k-truncated-svd","content":"Per-matrix cost is multiplied by the batch dimension product.  Constants\nmarked \"provisional\": iteration counts are input-dependent and the cubic\nconstant is the standard textbook estimate."},"1998":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-750","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"**Family rule** (DERIVED, radix-2 FFT — 5 real ops per butterfly):"},"1999":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-751","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"Op"},"2000":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-752","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"flop\\_cost"},"2001":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-753","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"basis"},"2002":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-754","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`fft.fft`, `fft.ifft`"},"2003":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-755","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`5 × N × ⌈log₂ N⌉`, `N` = transform length"},"2004":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-756","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"DERIVED: 5 real ops per butterfly"},"2005":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-757","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`fft.fft2`, `fft.ifft2`, `fft.fftn`, `fft.ifftn`"},"2006":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-758","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"staged: `Σ` of the per-axis 1-D `fft` costs over numpy's execution cascade (reverse axis order), each `5 × batchᵢ × dᵢ × ⌈log₂ dᵢ⌉` at that axis's *current* intermediate shape; reduces to `5 × N × Σᵢ⌈log₂ dᵢ⌉`, `N = prod(transform dims)`, when `s` resizes no axis"},"2007":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-759","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"DERIVED: sum of the 1-D cascade; the final-shape product holds only when no axis is resized by `s`"},"2008":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-760","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`fft.rfft`, `fft.irfft`"},"2009":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-761","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`5 × (N/2) × ⌈log₂ N⌉`"},"2010":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-762","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"DERIVED: real-input / real-output half-spectrum"},"2011":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-763","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`fft.rfft2`, `fft.irfft2`, `fft.rfftn`, `fft.irfftn`"},"2012":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-764","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"staged: a real 1-D FFT on the last axis (`5 × (d_last/2) × ⌈log₂ d_last⌉`) plus complex 1-D FFTs on the remaining axes over the half-spectrum intermediate, summed in numpy's order (r2c: real axis first; c2r: real axis last)"},"2013":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-765","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"DERIVED: per-stage cascade; the half-spectrum shrinks the downstream batch, so it differs from `5 × (N/2) × Σᵢ⌈log₂ dᵢ⌉` except in the no-resize two-axis case"},"2014":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-766","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`fft.hfft`"},"2015":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-767","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`5 × (n_out/2) × ⌈log₂ n_out⌉`"},"2016":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-768","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"DERIVED: hfft = irfft(conj(a)) — conjugate-symmetry halves the work"},"2017":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-769","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`fft.ihfft`"},"2018":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-770","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`5 × (n/2) × ⌈log₂ n⌉`"},"2019":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-771","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"DERIVED: same `hfft_cost(n)` formula"},"2020":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-772","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`fft.fftfreq`"},"2021":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-773","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`n` (index grid scaled by `1/(n*d)` — one divide per output element)"},"2022":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-774","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"DECLARED: `n` divides"},"2023":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-775","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`fft.rfftfreq`"},"2024":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-776","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`n//2 + 1` (real-spectrum grid has `n//2 + 1` elements)"},"2025":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-777","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"DECLARED: `n//2 + 1` divides"},"2026":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-778","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`fft.fftshift`, `fft.ifftshift`"},"2027":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-779","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"`numel(output)`"},"2028":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-780","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"DERIVED: `numpy.roll`-based data-movement reindex, billed at weight 1.0 like a materializing copy (not part of the FFT priced-in family) — see Copy and gather"},"2029":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-781","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"The table above gives the **per-transform** cost; every transform op also multiplies by\nhowever many independent transforms the call performs. `fft`/`ifft`/`rfft`/`irfft`\ntransform a single `axis` and batch over every other axis (`numel(input) / N`).\n`fft2`/`ifft2`/`rfft2`/`irfft2`/`fftn`/`ifftn`/`rfftn`/`irfftn` transform the given\n`axes` — or, when `axes` is omitted but `s` is given, the trailing `len(s)` axes, not\nevery axis — and batch over whatever axes remain."},"2030":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-782","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"**Complex dtypes**: `complex_factor` is **1** (priced in) — `5N·log₂N` already counts the\ncomplex transform's real FLOPs (10 per radix-2 butterfly), so a complex128 transform bills\nonly the `2.0` dtype rate over its float32 counterpart, with no extra structural factor\n(see FFT is priced in)."},"2031":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-783","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#fft","content":"All counted FFT ops use **weight 1.0**.  Source: `src/flopscope/numpy/fft/_transforms.py`."},"2032":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-784","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"Op"},"2033":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-785","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"flop\\_cost"},"2034":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-786","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"basis"},"2035":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-787","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"source"},"2036":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-788","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`polyval`"},"2037":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-789","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`2 × deg × points` (Horner: 1 mul + 1 add per coefficient per point, FMA=2)"},"2038":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-790","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"DERIVED"},"2039":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-791","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`_polynomial.py`"},"2040":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-792","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`polyfit`"},"2041":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-793","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`m×deg` (Vandermonde build) `+ lstsq_cost(m, deg+1, ncols)` (`ncols = y.shape[1]` for 2-D `y`, else 1)"},"2042":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-794","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"DERIVED: builds an `(m, deg+1)` Vandermonde matrix and solves it via `lstsq` (SVD least-squares) — prices the identical solve `linalg.lstsq` charges for the same shape, rather than a cheaper standalone normal-equations estimate; the SVD factorization is shared across `ncols` RHS columns, so cost grows sublinearly in `ncols`, not linearly"},"2043":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-795","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`_polynomial.py:polyfit_cost`"},"2044":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-796","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`polyadd`, `polysub`"},"2045":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-797","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"size of the axis-0 zero-padded, broadcast-aligned result (= `numpy.polyadd(a1, a2).size`; reduces to `max(n1, n2, 1)` for 1-D inputs)"},"2046":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-798","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"DERIVED: mirrors numpy's own zero-pad-then-broadcast algorithm on shapes, including higher-rank/broadcasting operands"},"2047":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-799","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`_polynomial.py`"},"2048":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-800","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`polymul`"},"2049":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-801","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`2nm − n − m` (direct conv, FMA=2)"},"2050":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-802","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"DERIVED"},"2051":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-803","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`_polynomial.py`"},"2052":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-804","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`polydiv`"},"2053":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-805","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`1 + Q·(2·n2 + 1)`, `Q = max(n1 − n2 + 1, 0)` (`n1` = dividend length, `n2` = divisor length; each of the `Q` quotient terms costs 1 scale-divide + `n2` mul + `n2` sub — not `polymul`'s `2nm − n − m`, though the two happen to coincide at some shapes)"},"2054":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-806","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"DERIVED"},"2055":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-807","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`_polynomial.py:polydiv_cost`"},"2056":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-808","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`convolve`"},"2057":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-809","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`full`: `2nm − n − m`; `valid`: `(2·min−1)·(max−min+1)`; `same`: exact dot-length sum per numpy C layout"},"2058":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-810","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"DERIVED per-mode"},"2059":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-811","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`_pointwise.py:convolve`"},"2060":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-812","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`poly` (1-D, build from roots)"},"2061":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-813","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`(3n² + n) // 2`, `n = len(roots)` (iterative convolution with length-2 kernel per root; FMA=2)"},"2062":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-814","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"DERIVED"},"2063":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-815","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`_polynomial.py:poly_cost`"},"2064":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-816","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`polyder`"},"2065":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-817","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`t × n − t(t+1)/2`, `t = min(m, n−1)` (order-aware; one multiply per surviving coefficient per derivative step)"},"2066":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-818","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"DERIVED"},"2067":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-819","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`_polynomial.py:polyder_cost`"},"2068":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-820","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`polyint`"},"2069":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-821","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`m × n + m(m−1)/2` (order-aware; m passes each dividing n+j coefficients)"},"2070":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-822","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"DERIVED"},"2071":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-823","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`_polynomial.py:polyint_cost`"},"2072":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-824","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`roots`"},"2073":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-825","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`10n³`, `n = stripped companion dimension` (zero-leading/trailing coefficients stripped before companion matrix is built)"},"2074":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-826","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"DERIVED: delegates to `eigvals_cost` on trimmed degree"},"2075":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-827","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`_polynomial.py:roots_cost`"},"2076":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-828","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"`polyfit`, `polyder`, and `polyint` return float64 regardless of the input dtype, so\ntheir real bill is `2×` the `flop_cost` above; `polyval` preserves the input dtype and\nbills at its own rate. `polydiv` preserves a float or complex input dtype but floors an\ninteger/bool input to float64 (numpy runs its scale-divide there), the same\nkind-conditional promotion an `eigvals`-backed linalg op uses."},"2077":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-829","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#polynomial","content":"Source: `src/flopscope/_polynomial.py`."},"2078":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-830","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"Random ops are composite: the generation kernel cost and any setup cost\n(PRNG state update, rejection sampling) are folded into `flop_cost`; the\nweight tier **varies** by distribution family.  The full bill follows the\nfour-factor formula — draws bill their output dtype's rate (numpy's samplers\ndefault to float64, rate 2.0); complex dtypes are covered below."},"2079":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-831","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"Weight tiers:"},"2080":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-832","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"**weight 1.0** — plain uniform/integer/structural draws: `rand`, `random`,\n`random_sample`, `ranf`, `sample` (a documented numpy alias of\n`random_sample` — the same uniform draw, so it prices at the same tier as its\ntwins, not at the reorder tier), `uniform`, `randint`, `integers`,\n`multivariate_normal`."},"2081":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-833","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"**weight 4.0** — reorder / resample ops, the same non-sequential access\ntier as sort and gather: `random.permutation`,\n`random.shuffle` and `random.choice` at module level, and every\n`Generator`/`RandomState`\n`.choice` / `.permutation` / `.shuffle` method (plus `Generator.permuted`). The module-level\nsurface delegates to the legacy `RandomState` singleton, so it runs the same\nselection machinery as the method surface and prices identically — a\nmodule-level entry at weight 1.0 would be a cheaper alias route around this\ntier. A Fisher-Yates\nreorder and a rejection-sampled choice both touch memory non-sequentially, the\nsame reason a sort does."},"2082":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-834","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"**weight 16.0** — transcendental samplers (every continuous/transformed\ndistribution): `normal`, `standard_normal`, `randn`, `exponential`,\n`standard_exponential`, `poisson`, `binomial`, `geometric`,\n`hypergeometric`, `multivariate_hypergeometric`, `negative_binomial`,\n`multinomial`, `beta`, `chisquare`, `noncentral_chisquare`, `dirichlet`,\n`f`, `noncentral_f`, `gamma`, `gumbel`, `laplace`,\n`logistic`, `lognormal`, `logseries`, `pareto`, `power`, `rayleigh`,\n`standard_cauchy`, `standard_gamma`, `standard_t`, `triangular`,\n`vonmises`, `wald`, `weibull`, `zipf`, and all their Generator /\nRandomState counterparts."},"2083":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-835","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"Op / family"},"2084":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-836","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"flop\\_cost"},"2085":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-837","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"weight"},"2086":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-838","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"basis"},"2087":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-839","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"source"},"2088":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-840","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"`random.rand`, `random.random`, `random.random_sample`, `random.ranf`"},"2089":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-841","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"`numel(output)`"},"2090":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-842","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-le