[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"`sum(colors) + 2 × num_variates × min(nsample, sum(colors) − nsample) + numel(output)`"},"2170":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-922","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"16.0"},"2171":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-923","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"DERIVED composite: numpy builds a temporary counting buffer of length `sum(colors)`, then for each of the `num_variates` output draws does a partial Fisher-Yates shuffle over `min(nsample, sum(colors) − nsample)` buffer entries (whichever of the sampled/excluded side is smaller), followed by a separate pass counting the colors of those same shuffled entries — two full passes over that length"},"2172":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-924","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"`_cost_formulas.py`"},"2173":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-925","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"`random.beta`, `random.chisquare`, `random.noncentral_chisquare`, `random.dirichlet`, `random.f`, `random.noncentral_f`, `random.gamma`, `random.gumbel`, `random.laplace`, `random.logistic`, `random.lognormal`, `random.logseries`, `random.pareto`, `random.power`, `random.rayleigh`, `random.standard_cauchy`, `random.standard_exponential`, `random.standard_gamma`, `random.standard_t`, `random.triangular`, `random.vonmises`, `random.wald`, `random.weibull`, `random.zipf`"},"2174":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-926","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"`numel(output)`"},"2175":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-927","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"16.0"},"2176":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-928","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"DECLARED: flop\\_cost = numel(output); transcendental weight 16.0 for all continuous/transformed distributions; each draw resolves to its actual output dtype — float64 (rate 2.0) for the continuous members, int64 (rate 2.0) for the discrete `logseries`/`zipf` — so the full bill is `32 × numel` in practice"},"2177":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-929","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"`_cost_formulas.py`"},"2178":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-930","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"**Complex dtypes**: the distribution samplers produce **real-only** outputs, so a complex\nresolved dtype is `complex_factor = \"illegal\"` and raises. A draw bills at its **output\ndtype's** rate — `standard_normal(dtype=float64)` bills `2×` the float32 draw. The\ndata-movement random ops (`shuffle`, `permutation`, `choice`, `Generator.permuted`) permute\ncaller-supplied values of any *numeric* dtype and are billed dtype-neutrally — their cost\ncounts the selection work, which is width-independent — so complex input does not raise. A\nnon-numeric pool (object included) is refused by the dtype ban before the selection work\never runs, see Dtype and precision."},"2179":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-931","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#random-module-level-generator-randomstate","content":"Source: `src/flopscope/numpy/random/_cost_formulas.py`."},"2180":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-932","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#stats","content":"Stats ops are composite (weight 1.0; all per-element factors in `flop_cost`).\nThe billed element count is the numel of the broadcast of the input with the\ndistribution parameters — array-valued `loc`/`scale` (or `a`/`b`/`s`) enlarge\nthe output beyond `x` and are charged accordingly. Every `stats.*` kernel computes\nin float64 regardless of the input's own dtype, so the real bill is `2×` the\nper-element counts below even for a float32 input (e.g. `stats.uniform.pdf` on a\n10-element float32 array bills `20`, not `10`)."},"2181":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-933","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#stats","content":"Op"},"2182":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-934","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#stats","content":"flop\\_cost (per element)"},"2183":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-935","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#stats","content":"basis"},"2184":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-936","page_id":"/docs/u