ocs/understanding/flop-counting-model#counting-diff-ediff1d-clip-allclose-isclose-count_nonzero-trace","content":"DERIVED: Gram term `f²` dot products of length `s` (2f²s) + centering pass `fs` elements × 2 FLOPs"},"2402":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1154","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#counting-diff-ediff1d-clip-allclose-isclose-count_nonzero-trace","content":"`_pointwise.py:_cov_cost`"},"2403":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1155","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#counting-diff-ediff1d-clip-allclose-isclose-count_nonzero-trace","content":"`corrcoef`"},"2404":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1156","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#counting-diff-ediff1d-clip-allclose-isclose-count_nonzero-trace","content":"`2f²s + 2fs + 2f² + f`"},"2405":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1157","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#counting-diff-ediff1d-clip-allclose-isclose-count_nonzero-trace","content":"DERIVED: cov\\_cost + normalization (f² divides, 2 FLOPs each, + f sqrts)"},"2406":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1158","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#counting-diff-ediff1d-clip-allclose-isclose-count_nonzero-trace","content":"`_pointwise.py:_corrcoef_cost`"},"2407":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1159","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#counting-diff-ediff1d-clip-allclose-isclose-count_nonzero-trace","content":"`unwrap`"},"2408":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1160","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#counting-diff-ediff1d-clip-allclose-isclose-count_nonzero-trace","content":"`13 × numel(input)`"},"2409":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1161","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#counting-diff-ediff1d-clip-allclose-isclose-count_nonzero-trace","content":"DERIVED: 13 charged one-FLOP passes (diff, sub×2, mod, add, cmp×3, bitwise-and, select×2, abs, cumsum); the 2 select passes (3-arg `where`) no longer discount to free now that 3-arg `where` itself bills, so all 13 passes are charged — prior value was 11"},"2410":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1162","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#counting-diff-ediff1d-clip-allclose-isclose-count_nonzero-trace","content":"`_unwrap.py:unwrap_cost`"},"2411":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1163","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#counting-diff-ediff1d-clip-allclose-isclose-count_nonzero-trace","content":"`cov` and `corrcoef` compute in float64 regardless of the input dtype, so their real\nbill is `2×` the formulas above."},"2412":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1164","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather","content":"**Family rule: every write is metered; the access *pattern* sets the weight.**"},"2413":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1165","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather","content":"This family used to be uniformly free (pure relocation/selection bought a 0 bill). It\nis not any more: a materializing copy writes real memory and is charged weight 1.0; a\ncomputed-index gather reads via a non-sequential pattern and is charged weight 4.0\n(the access tier); only genuine views stay free. `flop_cost`\nis the count of elements actually **written** — for the triangular/diagonal family\nthat is fewer than the whole array (e.g. `eye`'s off-diagonal zeros are free), not the\nwhole buffer indiscriminately."},"2414":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1166","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather","content":"**Materializing copy (weight 1.0, `flop_cost = numel(output)`)** — assembles a new\nbuffer by copying or rearranging existing values sequentially: `concatenate`, `stack`,\n`hstack`, `vstack`, `column_stack`, `dstack`, `block`, `bmat`, `tile`, `repeat`,\n`resize`, `roll`, `insert`, `append`, `delete`, `concat`, `fromiter` (materializes an\niterable into an array, one write per element). `row_stack` bills exactly `vstack`'s\ncost — it is a bare `return vstack(tup)` with no `deduct()` of its own, so its own\nweight entry is inert by construction. `reshape`, `ravel`, `copy`, and `require` are\nbilled `numel(input)` regardless of whether NumPy itself returns a view or a copy for\nthat call — see Views and metadata for why the\ncautious, always-charged price was chosen over trying to detect the view case.\n`flatten` (the ndarray method — there is no free-standing `fnp.flatten` function) is\nalways a genuine copy, never a view, and is billed the same `numel(input)` under the\n`copy` op name. `fft.fftshift`/`fft.ifftshift` are `numpy.roll` under the hood — a\ndata-movement reindex, not part of the FFT priced-in family (see FFT)."},"2415":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1167","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather","content":"**Triangular / diagonal writes (weight 1.0)** — bill only the cells that survive, not\nthe whole array:"},"2416":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1168","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather","content":"Op"},"2417":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1169","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather","content":"flop\\_cost"},"2418":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1170","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather","content":"basis"},"2419":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1171","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather","content":"`triu`"},"2420":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1172","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather","content":"elements at/above the kth diagonal (batch leading dims multiply in; floored at 1)"},"2421":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1173","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather","content":"DECLARED"},"2422":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1174","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather","content":"`tril`"},"2423":{"id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model-1175","page_id":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/understanding/flop-counting-model#copy-and-gather","content":"elements at/below the kth diagonal (batch leading dims mul