they are Python functions built on other ufuncs."],[1256,"The unifying philosophy — every byte written is metered"],[517,"The version string includes the installed NumPy version suffix. If you see a version number, flopscope is installed correctly."],[2707,"The wrapped function computes the analytical cost from input shapes"],[2664,"The wrapping pattern"],[2672,"The wrapping pattern that makes `import flopscope.numpy as fnp` the counted NumPy surface"],[727,"There are two separate symmetry declarations — one for inputs, one for outputs:"],[1397,"These are **current policy values, subject to tuning** — the same status as the weight\nladder, and configured in the same file (`src/flopscope/data/default_weights.json`, under\n`\"dtype_rates\"`). They are a deliberate competition-design choice, not a hardware\nmeasurement."],[412,"These are custom-cost ops, not free; check the cheat sheet"],[392,"These are pure functions — no `BudgetContext` needed."],[998,"These match `numpy.random` semantics exactly, plus FLOP accounting. **They share numpy's global RNG state**, which is fine for one-shot scripts but creates a foot-gun in longer-running programs — see Pitfall: global state below."],[2480,"These ops derive a selector by testing element values (`!= 0`), so the test is their\ncompute cost. The predicate and the selection are the *same* step here — unlike the\n3-arg `where(cond, x, y)` above, where the predicate (a separate charged op) is an\n*input*."],[2544,"These ops return coordinate/index arrays rather than values, and price\n**dtype-neutrally** — the billed count is `numel` of the returned index array(s)\nregardless of the array's actual integer dtype, since the work (computing positions)\ndoes not depend on how wide the position values happen to be stored:"],[1956,"These ops use LAPACK drivers that iterate until convergence; counts are\nleading-order estimates of the standard operation count.  All use\n**weight 1.0**, and bill `complex_factor = 4` on complex input (as for the\ndirect linalg family)."],[377,"These payloads include:"],[1341,"This closes a loophole where `x.astype(x.dtype)` (default `copy=True`) was a free\nsubstitute for `x.copy()`, and `x.astype(bool)` / `x.astype(int)` were free substitutes\nfor a billed `!= 0` test or a `trunc`-then-cast. It also means choosing a narrower\nworking dtype now costs a one-time `copy`-priced toll to get there; the arithmetic\nperformed *afterward* on the narrower array is still cheaper per the dtype-rate table\n(see Which dtype prices a call), so that saving is not\neliminated — only no longer free to acquire up front. Contrast the charged cases with\n`ones`/`full`, which also touch every element but write an actual value into memory —\nthose are charged the same way (see Views and metadata\ncategory 2 above and Copy and gather)."],[1136,"This declares one full symmetric group on axes `(0, 1)` and another on\n`(2, 3)`."],[1137,"This explicit group is useful once you want to inspect or combine symmetry directly."],[2413,"This family used to be uniformly free (pure relocation/selection bought a 0 bill). It\nis not any more: a materializing copy writes real memory and is charged weight 1.0; a\ncomputed-index gather reads via a non-sequential pattern and is charged weight 4.0\n(the access tier); only genuine views stay free. `flop_cost`\nis the count of elements actually **written** — for the triangular/diagonal family\nthat is fewer than the whole array (e.g. `eye`'s off-diagonal zeros are free), not the\nwhole buffer indiscriminately."],[516,"This gives you an editable install plus the `dev`, `docs`, and `sympy` extras."],[1144,"This helper is ideal for docs, tests, and experiments:"],[497,"This is also a local-vs-remote difference: the in-process `flopscope` backend is\na NumPy subclass and currently tolerates `arr[i] = ...`, but the remote backend\nAIcrowd grades on rejects it. Write the functional form so local runs and graded\nruns behave identically."],[1610,"This is deliberate